/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/google-ace.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("google-ace", [["ace1",0,1,"Your company wants every Finance project to inherit the same IAM grants and organization policies, while keeping those projects apart from Engineering's. What should you create?",["A folder for Finance under the organization, with the Finance projects moved into it","A separate Cloud Billing account that is used only by Finance projects and their resources","One large Finance project that holds every Finance workload","A label department=finance added to each Finance project"],0,"Folders group projects so IAM allow policies and organization policies set on the folder are inherited by every project in it. A billing account only pays for usage, and labels organize costs but carry no policy.","Resource hierarchy",[null,"A billing account only decides who pays for usage; it carries no IAM grants or organization policies for projects to inherit.","Putting every workload in one project removes the separation between workloads and makes least privilege and quotas harder to manage.","Labels help group costs and filter resources, but IAM and organization policies are never inherited through labels."],2],["ace2",0,1,"A developer created a project with the ID acme-web-prod. The team now wants it to be called acme-web-production everywhere. What is true?",["Both the project ID and the project name can be edited later on the project settings page","The display name can be changed, but the project ID is fixed after creation","The project number can be edited, but the project ID cannot","The ID can be changed after moving the project into a new folder"],1,"The project name is only a display label and can be changed. The project ID is globally unique and permanent, so a new ID means a new project. Project numbers are assigned by Google and can't be edited either.","Creating projects",["Only the display name can be edited; the project ID is permanent once the project exists.",null,"The project number is assigned by Google and can't be edited either, so this gets the permanent identifiers backwards.","Moving a project between folders changes its parent, not its ID; the ID stays fixed for the project's lifetime."],1],["ace3",0,1,"You try to deploy a Cloud Run service in a new project and get an error saying the API has not been used in the project before or is disabled. What should you do?",["Grant yourself the Owner role at the organization node so the API permissions are inherited by the project","Link the project to a second billing account","Enable the Cloud Run Admin API, for example with gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com","Create a custom mode VPC network in the project first"],2,"Most Google Cloud APIs must be enabled per project before use. More IAM power or another billing account doesn't enable the API, and Cloud Run doesn't need a VPC.","Enabling APIs",["IAM permissions don't turn an API on; the error is about the API being disabled in the project, and Owner at the organization is also far too broad.","A second billing account is not possible (a project links to one) and wouldn't enable the API anyway.",null,"Cloud Run doesn't need a VPC to deploy, so creating a network does nothing about a disabled API."],1],["ace4",0,1,"Security requires that nobody in the organization can create service account keys, including project Owners. What should you configure?",["A custom IAM role that leaves out iam.serviceAccountKeys.create","A VPC Service Controls perimeter around every project","A log sink that alerts on key creation events","The organization policy constraint that disables service account key creation"],3,"An organization policy constraint applies to everyone, whatever their IAM roles, and is inherited by all projects. A custom role doesn't stop an Owner, and a log alert only reports keys after they exist.","Organization Policy Service",["A custom role only limits people who are given that role; Owners and other broad roles still include the key creation permission.","VPC Service Controls protects data from being exfiltrated across a perimeter; it doesn't stop IAM service account keys from being created.","A sink alert only reports a key after it has already been created, so it detects instead of preventing.",null],2],["ace5",0,1,"Data residency rules say resources in the EU folder may only be created in EU locations. Which control is the most direct?",["The resource locations organization policy constraint set on the EU folder","An IAM deny policy on compute.instances.create for the EU folder","A budget alert scoped to projects in the EU folder","Firewall rules that block traffic to non-EU regions"],0,"The resource locations constraint (gcp.resourceLocations) limits where location-based resources can be created and is inherited by the folder's projects. A deny policy would block creation everywhere, and budgets and firewalls don't control placement.","Organization Policy Service",[null,"Denying instance creation blocks VMs everywhere in the folder, including in EU regions, and does nothing for other resource types.","Budget alerts watch spending, not where resources are created, and can't block anything.","Firewall rules filter network traffic; they have no control over which region a resource is created in."],2],["ace6",0,1,"Employees are already managed in Microsoft Active Directory. You want the same users and groups to exist for Google Cloud sign-in without re-creating them by hand. What should you use?",["Create each user manually on the project's IAM page","Google Cloud Directory Sync to provision them into Cloud Identity","Create one service account per employee and share keys","Workload Identity Federation configured for every employee"],1,"Google Cloud Directory Sync copies users and groups from Active Directory or LDAP into Cloud Identity or Google Workspace. IAM grants access to identities but doesn't create user accounts, and service accounts are for workloads, not people.","Managing users and groups",["IAM pages grant roles to existing identities; they can't create Cloud Identity user accounts, and manual creation is exactly what must be avoided.",null,"Service accounts are identities for workloads, and sharing their keys among employees breaks accountability and security.","Workload Identity Federation lets external workloads get Google credentials; it isn't a way to provision employee accounts from Active Directory."],2],["ace7",0,1,"Twelve analysts need the same BigQuery roles in three projects, and people join and leave the team often. What is the most manageable way to grant access?",["Grant the roles directly to each analyst's user account in each of the three projects, one by one","Create one service account key and share it with the analysts","Grant the roles to a Google group of analysts and manage the group's membership","Grant the analysts the basic Owner role at the organization level"],2,"Granting roles to a group means joiners and leavers are handled by changing membership, not by editing three IAM policies. Individual grants are hard to maintain, shared keys break accountability, and Owner breaks least privilege.","Granting IAM roles",["Individual grants in three projects must be edited every time someone joins or leaves, which is exactly the maintenance burden to avoid.","A shared key hides who did what, can leak, and gives everyone the service account's access with no per-person control.",null,"Owner at the organization grants full control of every project, far beyond the BigQuery access the analysts need."],1],["ace8",0,1,"A user has roles/viewer on a folder and roles/editor on a project inside that folder. What is their effective access on the project?",["Viewer only, because a grant on a folder always overrides a grant on a project below it","No access, because conflicting grants cancel each other out","Viewer until an organization administrator approves the Editor grant","Editor, because inherited and direct allow policy grants are combined"],3,"IAM allow policies are additive down the hierarchy: effective access is the union of grants on the project and its ancestors. Nothing overrides or cancels a grant except an IAM deny policy.","Resource hierarchy",["IAM allow policies don't override each other by level; grants on the folder and the project are combined.","Grants never cancel each other out; effective access is the union of all allow policy grants.","There is no approval step for IAM grants; a binding takes effect as soon as it is added.",null],2],["ace9",0,1,"A finance employee must link new projects to the company billing account but must not change the billing account's settings or payment details. Which role on the billing account fits best?",["Billing Account Administrator","Billing Account Viewer","Billing Account User","Billing Account Costs Manager"],2,"Billing Account User allows linking projects to the billing account (together with a project-level permission such as Project Billing Manager). Administrator could change settings, Viewer can't link projects, and Costs Manager manages budgets and exports.","Cloud Billing accounts",["Billing Account Administrator can change payment methods and settings, which the employee must not be able to do.","Billing Account Viewer can only view costs and transactions; it can't link projects to the account.",null,"Costs Manager manages budgets and cost exports; it isn't the role meant for linking projects to the billing account."],3],["ace10",0,1,"A startup wants email alerts when its monthly spend reaches 50%, 90% and 100% of $1,000. What should it create?",["A Cloud Monitoring uptime check on the billing console page with an email notification channel","A budget on the billing account with threshold rules at those percentages","A quota limit on each project's API usage","An organization policy that restricts spending"],1,"Cloud Billing budgets send alerts at the thresholds you set, based on actual or forecasted spend. Uptime checks probe endpoints, quotas limit resource counts, and organization policies don't track money.","Budgets and alerts",["Uptime checks test whether an endpoint responds; they don't track spending against an amount.",null,"Quotas limit resource counts or API rates; they don't send alerts at percentages of a dollar amount.","Organization policies restrict configurations such as locations; there is no organization policy that tracks or limits spending."],1],["ace11",0,1,"Which statement about Cloud Billing budgets is correct?",["Budgets send alerts at thresholds but do not cap or stop spending on their own","A budget automatically shuts down all billable resources when the budgeted amount is reached","Budgets can only cover a whole billing account, never selected projects","Budget alerts are only available with a paid Customer Care plan"],0,"Budgets notify; they don't stop resources. To act automatically you publish budget notifications to Pub/Sub and handle them with your own code. Budgets can be scoped to projects, services or labels.","Budgets and alerts",[null,"Budgets never shut down resources by themselves; automatic action requires Pub/Sub notifications and your own code.","Budgets can be scoped to selected projects, services or labels, not only the whole billing account.","Budgets and their alerts are part of Cloud Billing for every account; they don't require a paid support plan."],2],["ace12",0,1,"The finance team wants to analyze detailed daily Google Cloud costs by project and label using SQL. What should you configure?",["A log sink that sends Admin Activity audit logs to BigQuery","Budget notifications published to a Pub/Sub topic","A monthly download of the PDF invoice","Cloud Billing data export to a BigQuery dataset"],3,"Billing export to BigQuery writes detailed usage and cost rows, including project and label, that you can query with SQL. Audit logs record who changed what, not costs, and invoices lack the detail.","Budgets and alerts",["Admin Activity audit logs record who changed configuration, not how much anything cost.","Budget notifications report threshold crossings, not the detailed per-project and per-label cost rows needed for SQL analysis.","A PDF invoice is a monthly summary that can't be queried with SQL by day, project or label.",null],2],["ace13",0,1,"You work in dev-proj (us-central1) and prod-proj (europe-west1) every day and keep running commands against the wrong one. Which gcloud feature helps most?",["Named configurations, created with gcloud config configurations create and switched with activate","A separate Cloud Shell browser tab for each project, remembering which tab points at which project","Adding --quiet to every gcloud command","An organization policy that sets a default region"],0,"Each named configuration stores its own account, project, region and zone, and one command switches between them. --quiet only suppresses prompts, and organization policies don't set CLI defaults.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",[null,"Relying on remembering which tab is which is error-prone and does nothing to set project defaults in the CLI.","--quiet only suppresses interactive prompts; it doesn't change which project a command runs against.","Organization policies restrict resource configurations; they don't set gcloud CLI defaults on your machine."],1],["ace14",0,1,"After installing the Google Cloud CLI on a new laptop, which command walks you through signing in and choosing a default project?",["gcloud auth application-default revoke","gcloud components update","gcloud init","gcloud config list"],2,"gcloud init authorizes an account and creates or updates a configuration with a default project, and optionally a region and zone. components update upgrades the CLI, and config list only shows current settings.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",["This revokes Application Default Credentials used by client libraries; it doesn't sign you in or set a project.","components update upgrades the installed CLI components; it doesn't authorize an account or pick a project.",null,"config list only shows the current configuration's properties; it doesn't walk you through setup."],1],["ace15",0,1,"You need to set the default Compute Engine zone in the active gcloud configuration to us-east1-b. Which command does it?",["gcloud compute zones set us-east1-b","gcloud config set compute/zone us-east1-b","gcloud config set zone/default us-east1-b","gcloud init --zone=us-east1-b"],1,"Defaults are properties set with gcloud config set SECTION/PROPERTY VALUE, so compute/zone is the right property. The other commands don't exist in that form.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",["There is no gcloud compute zones set command; zones are set as a configuration property.",null,"zone/default is not a valid property; the correct section and property are compute/zone.","gcloud init is interactive setup and doesn't take a --zone flag to set the default zone this way."],1],["ace16",0,1,"A contractor must run gcloud and kubectl against your project from a locked-down laptop where they can't install software. What should they use?",["A shared Compute Engine VM with a public IP address and one SSH key that the whole team uses","A GPU-backed Cloud Workstations configuration","A portable copy of the gcloud CLI on a USB drive","Cloud Shell, which runs in the browser with gcloud and kubectl ready to use"],3,"Cloud Shell gives an authenticated browser-based shell with the CLI tools preinstalled and nothing to install locally. A shared VM and key break accountability, and the other options still need software or extra cost.","Cloud Shell and the console",["A shared VM and SSH key break accountability, expose a public IP, and are harder to secure than a per-user browser shell.","Cloud Workstations is a paid managed development environment, and a GPU adds cost for no benefit in running CLI commands.","Running a portable copy from a USB drive still means installing software on a locked-down laptop, which isn't allowed.",null],1],["ace17",0,1,"Creating 40 VMs in us-central1 fails with an error that the CPUS quota is exceeded. What should you do?",["Disable and re-enable the Compute Engine API","Request a higher CPUS quota for us-central1 on the Quotas page","Move the project into a different folder","Switch to Spot VMs, which don't count against any quota"],1,"Compute Engine CPU quotas are per region; you can see usage and request an increase in the console. Re-enabling the API or moving folders doesn't change quotas, and Spot VMs still have quotas.","Quotas",["Re-enabling the API doesn't change the regional CPUS quota that the error says was exceeded.",null,"Folders affect inherited policies, not per-project quotas, so moving the project changes nothing.","Spot VMs also count against quotas (including dedicated preemptible quotas), so this claim is false."],1],["ace18",0,1,"Which statement about the Google Cloud resource hierarchy is correct?",["A project can sit in two folders at once to inherit both sets of policies","Compute and storage resources can be created directly under the organization node without any project","Folders can hold projects and other folders, and each resource belongs to one project","Organization policies can be set only at the project level"],2,"The hierarchy is organization > folders (which can nest) > projects > resources, and each node has exactly one parent. Organization policies and IAM can be set at the organization, folder or project level.","Resource hierarchy",["Every project has exactly one parent, so it can't sit in two folders at once.","Resources such as VMs and buckets always belong to a project; they can't be created directly under the organization.",null,"Organization policies can be set on the organization, folders or projects, not only projects."],1],["ace19",0,1,"A team wants to see which Google Cloud costs belong to each application when several apps share projects. What should they apply to resources?",["Labels such as app=checkout, then group billing reports and exports by label","Network tags such as app-checkout on each VM","A separate billing account for each application, with projects re-linked whenever an app moves","Custom IAM roles named after each application"],0,"Labels are key-value pairs that flow into billing reports and the BigQuery billing export, so costs can be grouped by application. Network tags are for firewall rules and routes, not cost reporting.","Regions, zones and labels",[null,"Network tags are used to target firewall rules and routes; they don't appear in billing data for cost reporting.","Separate billing accounts per app add administrative overhead and don't work when apps share projects.","IAM roles control access and have nothing to do with how costs are attributed in billing reports."],1],["ace20",0,1,"A latency-sensitive app for users in Tokyo must keep running if one zone fails. Which placement fits?",["One VM in a single zone of asia-northeast1","Instances spread across several zones of the asia-northeast1 region","A multi-region Cloud Storage bucket and nothing else","Instances in us-central1 behind Cloud CDN"],1,"Zones are isolated failure domains within a region, so spreading instances across zones in a nearby region (asia-northeast1 is Tokyo) gives low latency and zonal resilience. One zone is a single point of failure, and a distant region adds latency.","Regions, zones and labels",["A single VM in one zone fails completely if that zone has an outage.",null,"A storage bucket can store data across regions but doesn't run the application itself.","us-central1 is in the United States, far from Tokyo users, and CDN caching doesn't make a dynamic app zone-resilient in Asia."],2],["ace21",0,1,"You need to review which principals have which roles on project web-prod. Which command shows the project's IAM allow policy?",["gcloud iam roles list --project=web-prod","gcloud projects describe web-prod","gcloud iam service-accounts list --project=web-prod","gcloud projects get-iam-policy web-prod"],3,"get-iam-policy returns the bindings (roles and members) on the project. roles list shows custom roles defined in the project, describe shows metadata, and service-accounts list shows accounts, not who has access.","Granting IAM roles",["This lists custom roles defined in the project, not who has been granted roles.","describe shows project metadata such as its name, number and state, not its IAM bindings.","This lists the project's service accounts, not the members and roles in its allow policy.",null],1],["ace22",0,2,"A team has a stateless containerized HTTP API with spiky traffic. They want to pay nothing when it's idle and manage no servers or clusters. Which service fits best?",["A Compute Engine managed instance group","A GKE Standard cluster","Cloud Run","Bare Metal Solution"],2,"Cloud Run runs stateless containers, scales with requests and can scale to zero, with no infrastructure to manage. A MIG or GKE Standard cluster keeps nodes running and needs management.","Choosing a compute option",["A MIG needs VMs to be running and managed, so it doesn't scale to zero or remove server management.","A GKE Standard cluster keeps nodes running and requires cluster management, which the team wants to avoid.",null,"Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers for specialized workloads, the opposite of serverless."],1],["ace23",0,2,"An application needs a specific Linux kernel module and a licensed third-party agent installed on the operating system. Which compute option fits?",["Compute Engine VMs","Cloud Run","App Engine standard environment","Cloud Run functions"],0,"Only Compute Engine gives full control of the OS and kernel. The serverless options run your code in a managed sandbox where you can't load kernel modules.","Choosing a compute option",[null,"Cloud Run runs containers in a managed sandbox where you can't load kernel modules or control the host OS.","App Engine standard runs code in language runtimes with no access to the underlying OS or kernel.","Cloud Run functions run small pieces of code in a managed environment with no control of the OS or kernel."],1],["ace24",0,2,"A batch rendering job can be interrupted and restarted, and lowest cost is the top priority. Which Compute Engine option should you choose?",["Sole-tenant nodes","Spot VMs","A three-year committed use discount","Memory-optimized machine types"],1,"Spot VMs are heavily discounted but can be preempted at any time, which suits fault-tolerant batch jobs. Sole-tenant nodes cost more, and a long commitment doesn't suit an occasional job.","Compute Engine instances",["Sole-tenant nodes give you dedicated hosts for licensing or isolation and cost more than regular VMs, not less.",null,"A three-year commitment suits steady, long-running use; it doesn't fit an occasional interruptible job and still costs more than Spot pricing.","Memory-optimized machines are for large in-memory workloads and are expensive; nothing here needs extra memory."],1],["ace25",0,2,"An application needs 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory, and no predefined machine type is close. How do you avoid paying for unused resources?",["Choose the next larger predefined machine type","Use an e2-micro shared-core machine type and let a managed instance group autoscale to cover the load","Attach a local SSD to add memory","Create a custom machine type with 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory"],3,"Custom machine types let you choose vCPUs and memory within the family's limits. A larger predefined type wastes money, an e2-micro is far too small, and local SSD is storage, not memory.","Compute Engine instances",["The next larger predefined type means paying for vCPUs or memory the application doesn't need.","An e2-micro is a tiny shared-core machine; scaling out many of them doesn't give one process 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory.","Local SSD is block storage, not RAM, so it can't add memory to a VM.",null],2],["ace26",0,2,"A VM needs a scratch cache with very high IOPS and low latency, and losing that data when the VM stops is acceptable. Which storage should you attach?",["Local SSD","A standard persistent disk","A Cloud Storage bucket mounted with Cloud Storage FUSE","A regional persistent disk"],0,"Local SSDs are physically attached to the host for very high performance, but their data doesn't persist through a VM stop or host failure. Network disks are durable but slower, and Cloud Storage is object storage.","Compute Engine disks",[null,"A standard persistent disk is HDD-backed network storage, durable but far slower than what a high-IOPS cache needs.","Cloud Storage FUSE mounts object storage, which has much higher latency than block storage and is a poor fit for a hot cache.","A regional persistent disk adds durability across zones and some write latency, which isn't needed for disposable scratch data."],2],["ace27",0,2,"A VM's data disk must survive a zonal outage so the workload can be restarted in another zone of the same region without losing recent writes. What should you use?",["A local SSD","A regional persistent disk replicated across two zones","A zonal standard persistent disk with a daily snapshot","An ephemeral external IP address"],1,"Regional persistent disks replicate synchronously to two zones in a region, so a VM in the other zone can attach it after a failure. A daily snapshot loses writes since the last snapshot, and local SSD data doesn't survive.","Compute Engine disks",["Local SSD data doesn't survive the VM stopping or a host failure, so it can't be moved to another zone.",null,"A daily snapshot loses every write since the last snapshot, which breaks the no-data-loss requirement.","An IP address has nothing to do with disk replication or preserving data across zones."],2],["ace28",0,2,"Web servers must be recreated automatically when a health check fails, and more VMs must be added as CPU usage rises. What should you create?",["An unmanaged instance group of hand-built VMs","A snapshot schedule on each boot disk","A managed instance group from an instance template, with autoscaling and autohealing","A sole-tenant node group with CPU overcommit and a startup script that restarts the web server process"],2,"Managed instance groups create identical VMs from a template, autoscale on signals such as CPU, and recreate unhealthy VMs through autohealing. Unmanaged groups do neither.","Managed instance groups",["Unmanaged instance groups hold existing VMs but can't autoscale or recreate unhealthy instances automatically.","Snapshot schedules back up disks; they don't add VMs or replace failed ones.",null,"Sole-tenant nodes provide dedicated hosts, and a startup script only runs at boot; neither adds capacity or replaces failed VMs automatically."],2],["ace29",0,2,"You need to change the machine type used by an existing managed instance group. What is the right approach?",["Edit the machine type in the current instance template","Create a new instance template and start a rolling update of the group to it","Stop each VM and change its machine type one by one","Delete the managed instance group and recreate it from the same instance template with more VMs"],1,"Instance templates are immutable, so you create a new template and use a rolling update to replace VMs gradually. Changing VMs by hand drifts from the template, and recreating from the same template changes nothing.","Managed instance groups",["Instance templates are immutable, so the machine type in an existing template can't be edited.",null,"Changing VMs by hand makes them drift from the template, and the MIG may recreate them with the old machine type.","Recreating from the same template gives the same old machine type and causes an outage while the group is rebuilt."],2],["ace30",0,2,"A team wants to run containers on GKE without managing nodes, node pools or node sizes, paying mainly for what pods request. Which option fits?",["GKE Standard with one large node pool","Compute Engine VMs running Container-Optimized OS","GKE Standard with the cluster autoscaler turned off","A GKE Autopilot cluster"],3,"In Autopilot, Google manages the nodes and billing is based mainly on pod resource requests. Standard clusters make you size and pay for nodes, and plain VMs aren't Kubernetes at all.","GKE clusters",["In GKE Standard you still size, manage and pay for the node pool's VMs, which the team wants to avoid.","Plain VMs running Container-Optimized OS aren't Kubernetes and leave all node and scheduling management to you.","Turning off the autoscaler in Standard mode means even more manual node management, not less.",null],1],["ace31",0,2,"A production GKE cluster's control plane must stay available if one zone fails. What should you create?",["A regional cluster, which replicates the control plane across zones","A zonal cluster with a larger node pool","A zonal cluster with node auto-repair enabled","A zonal cluster with a private control plane endpoint"],0,"Regional clusters run control plane replicas and nodes across multiple zones in a region. A zonal cluster has a single control plane in one zone, whatever its node settings.","GKE clusters",[null,"A zonal cluster has one control plane in one zone, so a bigger node pool doesn't keep it available if that zone fails.","Node auto-repair fixes unhealthy nodes but doesn't protect the single zonal control plane from a zone outage.","A private endpoint changes how the control plane is reached, not how many zones it runs in."],1],["ace32",0,2,"Some workloads in a GKE Standard cluster need GPUs, while others run fine on general-purpose machines. What should you do?",["Replace every node in the cluster with GPU machines","Create a second cluster in another project","Add a GPU node pool and target it with node selectors or taints and tolerations","Run the GPU pods as a DaemonSet"],2,"Node pools let one cluster mix machine types, and scheduling controls send GPU pods to the GPU pool. Making every node a GPU node wastes money, and a DaemonSet runs a pod on every node.","GKE clusters",["Making every node a GPU node wastes money on workloads that don't need GPUs.","A second cluster in another project adds management overhead when a node pool in the same cluster solves this.",null,"A DaemonSet runs one pod on every node, which doesn't target GPU machines and isn't how you schedule selected workloads."],2],["ace33",0,2,"You created a Deployment named web whose containers listen on port 8080. Which command exposes it to the internet on port 80 through an external load balancer?",["kubectl expose deployment web --type=ClusterIP --port=80 --target-port=8080 --name=web-public","kubectl expose deployment web --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=8080","kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=3","kubectl port-forward deployment/web 80:8080"],1,"A Service of type LoadBalancer makes GKE provision an external load balancer with a public IP. ClusterIP is internal only, and port-forward is a temporary tunnel from your own machine.","Deploying workloads to GKE",["A ClusterIP Service is reachable only inside the cluster, whatever name you give it, so it isn't exposed to the internet.",null,"Scaling changes the number of replicas; it doesn't expose the Deployment on an external IP.","port-forward creates a temporary tunnel from your own machine, not an internet-facing load balancer."],2],["ace34",0,2,"Your team wants to define projects, VPCs and VMs as version-controlled code, preview changes before applying them, and reuse modules. What should you use?",["Commands copied from Cloud Shell history into a wiki","Instance templates exported from the console","Snapshot schedules attached to each disk","Terraform with the Google Cloud provider, using plan and apply"],3,"Terraform describes infrastructure declaratively, shows a plan of changes before apply, and supports reusable modules and state. Saved commands aren't declarative, and templates or schedules cover only one resource type.","Infrastructure as code",["Copied commands are imperative, can't show a preview of changes, and aren't reusable modules.","Instance templates describe only VMs; they can't define projects or VPCs or preview changes across them.","Snapshot schedules create disk backups; they don't define infrastructure at all.",null],1],["ace35",0,2,"An image-processing step must run every time a file is uploaded to a Cloud Storage bucket. Which approach needs the least code and infrastructure?",["A Cloud Run function triggered by the bucket's object finalized event","A VM that polls the bucket every minute","A GKE CronJob that lists the bucket every five minutes","A Cloud Scheduler job that runs every hour"],0,"Event-driven functions (through Eventarc) run once per upload with no servers to manage. Polling and scheduled jobs add latency, cost and code that has to track which files were processed.","Cloud Run services and functions",[null,"A polling VM runs and costs money all the time, adds delay, and needs code to track which files were already processed.","A CronJob needs a GKE cluster and still polls, adding delay and code to avoid reprocessing files.","An hourly scheduled job adds up to an hour of delay and has to work out which files are new."],2],["ace36",0,2,"A Cloud Run service should accept requests only from specific internal services that call it with their own service accounts, not from anonymous users. What should you configure?",["Allow unauthenticated invocations and keep the URL secret","Set the maximum number of instances to 1","Require authentication and grant Cloud Run Invoker only to the callers' service accounts","Deploy the service to a region that has no public endpoint and share its URL only with the calling teams"],2,"With authentication required, callers must present an identity token, and only principals with roles/run.invoker can call the service. A hidden URL is not access control, and scaling settings or regions don't restrict callers.","Cloud Run services and functions",["Unauthenticated invocations let anyone on the internet call the service; a secret URL is not access control.","Maximum instances controls scaling and cost, not who is allowed to call the service.",null,"Cloud Run services get a public URL in every region, and sharing the URL carefully is still not access control."],3],["ace37",0,2,"A global retail app needs a relational database with strong consistency, horizontal scaling and multi-region availability. Which product fits?",["Cloud SQL for MySQL","Spanner","Bigtable","Firestore"],1,"Spanner is a horizontally scalable relational database with strong consistency and multi-region configurations. Cloud SQL is regional and scales up, while Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL.","Choosing a data product",["Cloud SQL is regional and scales vertically and with read replicas; it can't provide strongly consistent multi-region writes at global scale.",null,"Bigtable is NoSQL with no relational schema or SQL transactions across rows as the app requires.","Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not a relational database with SQL transactions."],2],["ace38",0,2,"A regional web app with moderate traffic needs a managed PostgreSQL database that works with existing SQL tools. Which product fits with the least effort?",["Bigtable","BigQuery","Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL","Memorystore for Redis"],2,"Cloud SQL is managed MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server for typical transactional apps. BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, Bigtable is NoSQL, and Memorystore is an in-memory cache.","Choosing a data product",["Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column store and doesn't speak PostgreSQL or support standard SQL tools.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not a transactional database for a web app.",null,"Memorystore for Redis is an in-memory cache, not a durable PostgreSQL database."],1],["ace39",0,2,"An IoT platform ingests millions of time-series writes per second and reads them by device ID and timestamp with very low latency. Which product fits?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL for MySQL","BigQuery","Filestore"],0,"Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database built for massive throughput and low-latency key-based access, such as time series. Cloud SQL can't scale writes like this, and BigQuery is for analytics, not serving.","Choosing a data product",[null,"Cloud SQL scales up rather than out and can't absorb millions of writes per second.","BigQuery is built for analytical scans, not for serving low-latency lookups by key at high write rates.","Filestore provides NFS file shares, not a database for time-series reads and writes."],2],["ace40",0,2,"A company needs encrypted connectivity between its data center and a VPC within days, over the public internet, with dynamic routing. What should it deploy?",["Dedicated Interconnect","Cloud NAT","Direct Peering","HA VPN with a Cloud Router using BGP"],3,"HA VPN creates IPsec tunnels over the internet, and Cloud Router exchanges routes with BGP. Dedicated Interconnect is a private physical connection that takes longer to provision, Cloud NAT is outbound internet access, and Direct Peering reaches Google services, not your VPC.","Hybrid connectivity",["Dedicated Interconnect is a private physical connection that takes much longer to provision and doesn't run over the internet.","Cloud NAT gives private VMs outbound internet access; it doesn't connect a data center to a VPC.","Direct Peering connects to Google's public services such as Workspace, not privately to your VPC.",null],2],["ace41",0,2,"Audit files are written once, read maybe once a year, and must be kept for at least 7 years at the lowest storage cost. Which Cloud Storage class fits?",["Standard","Nearline","Coldline","Archive"],3,"Archive has the lowest storage price and a 365-day minimum storage duration, which suits data read less than once a year. Nearline and Coldline cost more to store, and Standard is for frequent access.","Cloud Storage buckets",["Standard has no minimum duration but the highest storage price, which suits frequently accessed data, not a 7-year archive.","Nearline suits data read about once a month and costs more to store than Archive.","Coldline suits data read about once a quarter; Archive is cheaper for data read less than once a year.",null],1],["ace42",0,2,"A bucket should move objects to Coldline 90 days after creation and delete them after three years, automatically. What should you configure?",["Object Lifecycle Management rules on the bucket","A Cloud Scheduler job that runs gcloud storage mv","Object Versioning on the bucket","A retention policy locked with Bucket Lock"],0,"Lifecycle rules can set the storage class and delete objects based on age and other conditions. A retention policy prevents deletion before a time rather than causing it, and versioning keeps old versions.","Cloud Storage buckets",[null,"A scheduled script is extra code to maintain, while lifecycle rules do this natively and reliably.","Versioning keeps old versions of objects; it doesn't change storage classes or delete objects by age.","A retention policy prevents deletion until a time has passed; it doesn't move or delete objects automatically."],1],["ace43",0,2,"Users sometimes overwrite or delete important objects in a bucket, and you need to recover earlier versions. What should you enable?",["Uniform bucket-level access","Object Versioning","Requester Pays","Autoclass"],1,"With Object Versioning, an overwritten or deleted object becomes a noncurrent version that you can restore. The other settings control permissions, who pays for access, and automatic class changes.","Cloud Storage buckets",["Uniform bucket-level access changes how permissions are managed; it doesn't keep previous object versions.",null,"Requester Pays makes the reader pay for access charges; it has nothing to do with recovery.","Autoclass changes storage classes based on access patterns; it doesn't preserve overwritten or deleted data."],1],["ace44",0,2,"You need a VPC where you pick exactly which regions have subnets and which ranges they use, to avoid overlapping on-premises networks in 10.128.0.0/9. Which should you create?",["An auto mode VPC","The default network created with the project","A custom mode VPC with subnets you define","A legacy network"],2,"Custom mode VPCs have only the subnets and ranges you create. Auto mode (and the default network) create a subnet in every region from 10.128.0.0/9, which would overlap here, and legacy networks aren't recommended.","VPC networks",["An auto mode VPC creates subnets in every region from 10.128.0.0/9, which would overlap the on-premises range.","The default network is an auto mode VPC, so it has the same overlapping ranges and a subnet in every region.",null,"Legacy networks are deprecated, have no subnets and aren't recommended for new designs."],1],["ace45",0,2,"VMs with the network tag web must accept HTTP and HTTPS from the internet, and nothing else should be opened. Which firewall rule fits?",["Egress allow tcp:80,443 to 0.0.0.0/0 for all instances","Ingress allow tcp:80,443 from 0.0.0.0/0 with target tag web","Ingress allow all protocols from 0.0.0.0/0 with target tag web","Ingress deny tcp:80,443 from 0.0.0.0/0 at priority 100"],1,"An ingress allow rule for those two ports from any source, scoped by target tag, opens only what's needed. Egress rules control outbound traffic, allowing all protocols is too broad, and a deny rule does the opposite.","VPC networks",["Egress rules control outbound traffic; they don't allow inbound HTTP and HTTPS from users.",null,"Allowing all protocols from anywhere exposes every port on the VMs, which breaks the requirement to open nothing else.","A deny rule blocks exactly the traffic that must be allowed."],2],["ace46",0,2,"A central network team wants to manage one VPC's subnets and firewall rules, while app teams create VMs in their own projects on those subnets. What should they use?",["VPC Network Peering between the network team's VPC and a separate VPC in every application project","Cloud VPN tunnels between each pair of projects","Private Service Connect endpoints","Shared VPC, with a host project and attached service projects"],3,"Shared VPC lets service projects use subnets from a host project whose network is administered centrally. Peering connects separately managed VPCs, and VPN or Private Service Connect don't share subnets.","VPC networks",["Peering connects VPCs that are still administered separately, so each app team would manage its own network instead of the central team.","VPN tunnels between projects are complex, add cost and still leave every project with its own separately managed network.","Private Service Connect exposes specific services privately; it doesn't let teams create VMs in a centrally managed subnet.",null],2],["ace47",0,2,"A global HTTPS web app needs one anycast IP address, URL-based routing to different backends and Cloud CDN. Which load balancer fits?",["Global external Application Load Balancer","Internal passthrough Network Load Balancer","External passthrough Network Load Balancer","Regional internal proxy Network Load Balancer"],0,"The global external Application Load Balancer is a layer 7 proxy with one global anycast IP, URL maps and Cloud CDN integration. Network load balancers work at layer 4 without URL routing, and internal ones aren't internet-facing.","Load balancing",[null,"An internal passthrough Network Load Balancer serves traffic inside a VPC and can't give a public anycast IP or URL routing.","External passthrough Network Load Balancers are regional layer 4 balancers with no URL-based routing or Cloud CDN integration.","An internal proxy Network Load Balancer is regional, internal and layer 4, so it isn't internet-facing and can't route by URL."],2],["ace48",0,2,"VMs with no external IP addresses must download OS updates from the internet but must not be reachable from it. What should you configure?",["An ephemeral external IP on each VM","A global external Application Load Balancer","Cloud NAT on a Cloud Router in that region","VPC Network Peering with the default network"],2,"Cloud NAT gives instances without external IPs outbound internet access while allowing no unsolicited inbound connections. External IPs expose the VMs, and a load balancer handles inbound traffic.","Cloud NAT",["External IPs make each VM directly reachable from the internet, which the requirement forbids.","A load balancer handles inbound requests to backends; it doesn't give private VMs outbound internet access.",null,"Peering with another VPC doesn't provide a path to the internet for VMs without external IPs."],2],["ace49",0,3,"You must SSH to a VM that has no external IP address, without opening port 22 to the internet or running a bastion host. What should you use?",["Cloud NAT on the VM's subnet","gcloud compute ssh with --tunnel-through-iap, and a rule allowing IAP's range 35.235.240.0/20 on tcp:22","Add a temporary external IP to the VM and a firewall rule allowing tcp:22 from 0.0.0.0/0, then remove them","VPC Network Peering with Cloud Shell's network"],1,"IAP TCP forwarding tunnels SSH through Identity-Aware Proxy, so only IAP's range needs to reach port 22 and access is controlled by IAM. Cloud NAT is outbound only, and opening 22 to the world is what you're avoiding.","Managing Compute Engine",["Cloud NAT only handles outbound connections from private VMs; it can't carry an inbound SSH session to them.",null,"Opening port 22 to the whole internet, even temporarily, is exactly what the requirement rules out.","Cloud Shell doesn't live in a VPC you can peer with, so peering can't give it a private path to the VM."],2],["ace50",0,3,"Before a risky OS upgrade on a VM, you want a point-in-time copy of its boot disk that you can restore from. What should you do?",["Create an instance template from the VM","Enable autohealing on the VM","Take a snapshot of the persistent disk","Resize the disk to twice its size"],2,"A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a disk that you can restore to a new disk. Templates describe configuration, not data, and autohealing recreates VMs without restoring old data.","Snapshots and images",["An instance template stores VM settings such as machine type and image, not the current data on the disk.","Autohealing recreates a VM that fails a health check; it doesn't keep a copy of the disk's current data.",null,"Resizing a disk adds capacity but creates no backup copy to restore from."],1],["ace51",0,3,"You need daily backups of a VM's disks, kept for 14 days, without writing scripts. What should you configure?",["A snapshot schedule attached to the disks","A cron job on the VM that copies files to a bucket","A lifecycle rule on a Cloud Storage bucket","A single machine image created today"],0,"Snapshot schedules create snapshots on a schedule and delete them after the retention period. A cron job is a script to maintain, and a single machine image isn't a recurring backup.","Snapshots and images",[null,"A cron job is a script to write and maintain, which the requirement rules out, and it copies files rather than whole disks.","Lifecycle rules manage objects already in a bucket; they don't create backups of VM disks.","One machine image is a single backup, not daily backups with a 14-day retention."],1],["ace52",0,3,"You built a hardened OS configuration on one VM and want to create many identical VMs from it. What should you create from its boot disk?",["A snapshot schedule","A local SSD","A zonal reservation","A custom image, optionally added to an image family"],3,"Custom images are templates for boot disks, and image families let you always use the latest image in the family. Reservations hold capacity, and a local SSD is scratch storage.","Snapshots and images",["A snapshot schedule backs up disks over time; it isn't the usual template for creating many identical VMs.","Local SSD is temporary scratch storage and can't capture or copy a configured boot disk.","A reservation holds VM capacity in a zone; it contains no OS or software configuration.",null],2],["ace53",0,3,"Pods in a GKE Standard cluster often stay Pending because the default node pool is full. The pool should add nodes automatically, up to 10. Which command enables that?",["kubectl autoscale deployment web --max=10","gcloud container clusters update prod --enable-autoscaling --node-pool=default-pool --min-nodes=1 --max-nodes=10","gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling on the managed instance group behind the node pool, with max 10","kubectl scale nodes --replicas=10"],1,"The cluster autoscaler is enabled per node pool with gcloud container clusters update (or node-pools update). kubectl autoscale creates a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for pods, not nodes, and GKE's node groups shouldn't be autoscaled directly.","Managing GKE",["kubectl autoscale creates a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler that changes pod replicas, not the number of nodes.",null,"GKE manages the instance groups behind node pools, and autoscaling them directly conflicts with GKE; use the cluster autoscaler instead.","There's no kubectl scale for nodes; nodes are VMs managed by GKE, not a scalable Kubernetes workload."],1],["ace54",0,3,"A Deployment named shop runs 3 replicas and must run 8 during a flash sale starting now. Which command does it?",["gcloud container clusters resize shop-cluster --num-nodes=8","kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop","kubectl set image deployment/shop shop=shop:8","kubectl scale deployment/shop --replicas=8"],3,"kubectl scale changes the Deployment's replica count immediately. Resizing the cluster changes nodes, not pods, and rollout undo or set image change versions.","Managing GKE",["Resizing the cluster changes the number of nodes, not how many replicas the Deployment runs.","rollout undo reverts to an earlier revision; it doesn't change the replica count.","set image changes the container image version, and shop:8 is just a tag, not a replica count.",null],1],["ace55",0,3,"After you roll out a new image to the Deployment web, error rates spike. How do you return to the previous version quickly?",["kubectl delete deployment web","gcloud container clusters upgrade prod","kubectl rollout undo deployment/web","kubectl drain the nodes that run web"],2,"rollout undo reverts the Deployment to its previous ReplicaSet revision. Deleting it causes an outage, a cluster upgrade changes the Kubernetes version, and draining just moves pods.","Managing GKE",["Deleting the Deployment removes the app entirely, causing an outage instead of a rollback.","A cluster upgrade changes the Kubernetes version of the cluster, not the app's image.",null,"Draining nodes moves pods elsewhere but they keep running the same faulty image."],1],["ace56",0,3,"You deployed a new revision of a Cloud Run service and want 10% of traffic to go to it while 90% stays on the current revision. What should you do?",["Deploy a second service and use DNS round robin","Use gcloud run services update-traffic to give the new revision 10%","Set the new revision's maximum instances to 10","Delete the old revision after one hour"],1,"Cloud Run can split traffic between revisions of one service by percentage. DNS round robin can't weight traffic precisely, and instance limits don't control traffic share.","Managing Cloud Run",["DNS round robin can't weight traffic precisely and would require running and managing a second service.",null,"Maximum instances caps scaling for the revision; it doesn't set the share of traffic it receives.","Deleting the old revision removes the rollback option and doesn't create a 90/10 split."],2],["ace57",0,3,"Users complain that the first request to a rarely used Cloud Run service is slow because of cold starts. Which setting reduces this?",["Lower the maximum number of instances","Set concurrency to 1","Deploy to a region with fewer users","Set a minimum number of instances above zero"],3,"Minimum instances keep some container instances warm, so requests don't wait for startup (you pay for them while idle). Lowering max instances or concurrency tends to cause more startups, not fewer.","Managing Cloud Run",["Lowering maximum instances limits scaling and can increase queuing; it doesn't keep instances warm.","Concurrency 1 makes each instance handle one request at a time, which causes more new instances and more cold starts.","Region choice affects network latency to users, not container startup time.",null],2],["ace58",0,3,"You need to copy 2 TB of files from an Amazon S3 bucket into Cloud Storage every night, as a managed service. What should you use?",["Storage Transfer Service","gcloud storage cp run from Cloud Shell","Transfer Appliance","BigQuery Data Transfer Service"],0,"Storage Transfer Service moves data from other clouds, HTTP sources or buckets on a schedule. Cloud Shell sessions are temporary, Transfer Appliance is a physical device for offline transfer, and BigQuery Data Transfer Service loads data into BigQuery.","Managing Cloud Storage",[null,"A Cloud Shell session is temporary and interactive, so it isn't a managed, scheduled way to move 2 TB every night.","Transfer Appliance is a physical device for one-off offline transfers, not nightly copies from another cloud.","BigQuery Data Transfer Service loads data into BigQuery tables, not files into Cloud Storage buckets."],1],["ace59",0,3,"Which command lists the objects in the bucket gs://acme-logs?",["gcloud storage buckets create gs://acme-logs","gcloud compute disks list --filter=acme-logs","gcloud storage ls gs://acme-logs","gcloud projects describe acme-logs"],2,"gcloud storage ls lists buckets or the objects in a bucket. The other commands create a bucket, list disks, or describe a project.","Managing Cloud Storage",["This creates a new bucket named acme-logs; it doesn't list any objects.","This lists Compute Engine persistent disks, which are a different storage product from Cloud Storage buckets.",null,"This describes a project named acme-logs, not the contents of a bucket."],1],["ace60",0,3,"A Cloud SQL instance must fail over automatically to another zone if its primary zone goes down. What should you configure?",["A read replica created in the same zone as the primary, promoted by hand during an outage","High availability (regional) configuration with a standby in another zone","Automated backups only","Point-in-time recovery only"],1,"Cloud SQL high availability keeps a synchronously updated standby in a second zone and fails over automatically. Backups and point-in-time recovery need a manual restore, and a same-zone replica doesn't protect against a zone outage.","Operating databases",["A replica in the same zone doesn't help when that zone fails, and manual promotion isn't automatic failover.",null,"Backups require a manual restore to a new or existing instance, so they don't fail over automatically.","Point-in-time recovery restores data to a moment in time; it doesn't keep a standby ready in another zone."],1],["ace61",0,3,"Reporting queries are slowing down a Cloud SQL primary. You want to move read-only traffic elsewhere. What should you add?",["A high availability standby to serve the reads","Longer automated backup retention","A second database user for the reports","A read replica for the reporting queries"],3,"Read replicas serve read-only queries and take load off the primary. The HA standby can't serve traffic, and backup retention or extra users don't add capacity.","Operating databases",["The HA standby only takes over on failover and doesn't serve any read traffic.","Backup retention affects recovery options, not how much query load the primary carries.","An extra database user still runs its queries on the same primary, so the load doesn't move.",null],1],["ace62",0,3,"Before running a large BigQuery query, you want to know how many bytes it will process to estimate on-demand cost. What should you do?",["Add LIMIT 10, which reduces the bytes processed","Do a dry run (bq query --dry_run or the console's query validator)","Export the table to Cloud Storage first","Run SELECT * once to warm the cache"],1,"A dry run validates the query and reports the bytes it would process without charging. LIMIT doesn't reduce bytes scanned on a normal table, and SELECT * scans every column.","Operating databases",["LIMIT only trims the rows returned; BigQuery still scans the full columns of a normal table, so bytes processed don't drop.",null,"Exporting the table costs time and storage and doesn't tell you what the query will process.","SELECT * scans every column, which is the most expensive way to query and doesn't estimate anything."],1],["ace63",0,3,"Subnet 10.10.0.0/24 is running out of addresses, and you need more without recreating the VMs in it. What should you do?",["Shrink the range to /25 and recreate it","Convert the VPC to auto mode","Expand the primary range to a larger prefix such as /22 with expand-ip-range","Create a second VPC and peer it with the first"],2,"gcloud compute networks subnets expand-ip-range can grow a subnet's primary range in place, as long as it doesn't overlap other ranges. Ranges can't be shrunk, and a new VPC doesn't add addresses to this subnet.","Operating networks",["Subnet ranges can't be shrunk, and a /25 would give fewer addresses, not more.","A custom mode VPC can't be converted to auto mode, and auto mode wouldn't enlarge this subnet anyway.",null,"A new VPC doesn't add addresses to the existing subnet, and new VMs would have to be placed in a different network."],2],["ace64",0,3,"A VM's ephemeral external IP changes whenever the VM is stopped, which breaks a partner's allowlist. What should you do?",["Promote the address to a static external IP, or reserve one and assign it","Use only the VM's internal IP address","Put the VM behind Cloud NAT","Create a DNS record for the VM with a TTL of 0 and ask the partner to allowlist the name"],0,"A static external IP stays reserved to your project and doesn't change when the VM stops. You can promote the current ephemeral address to keep it. Cloud NAT is for outbound traffic, and DNS doesn't fix an IP-based allowlist.","Operating networks",[null,"The partner connects over the internet, so the VM's internal IP isn't reachable from their side.","Cloud NAT handles outbound connections from private VMs and doesn't give the VM a fixed inbound address.","An IP-based allowlist compares addresses, not names, so a DNS record doesn't stop the address from changing."],1],["ace65",0,3,"You want an email when average CPU on a group of VMs stays above 80% for 5 minutes. What should you create?",["A log sink that routes the VMs' syslog entries to a Pub/Sub topic read by a mail script","A budget alert at 80%","An uptime check against the VMs","A Cloud Monitoring alerting policy on CPU utilization with an email channel"],3,"Alerting policies watch metric conditions over a duration and notify channels such as email. Uptime checks test availability, budgets track spend, and log sinks route logs.","Cloud Monitoring",["CPU utilization is a metric, not a syslog entry, and a mail script behind Pub/Sub is custom work that alerting policies do natively.","Budget alerts track spending against a budget, not VM CPU usage.","Uptime checks test whether endpoints respond; they don't measure CPU usage over time.",null],1],["ace66",0,3,"You need memory usage and disk space metrics from Compute Engine VMs, which aren't collected by default. What should you install?",["The Cloud Trace client library","The Ops Agent","Cloud Profiler","An uptime check"],1,"The Ops Agent collects guest OS metrics such as memory and disk usage, plus logs. Default VM metrics come from the hypervisor and don't see inside the OS. Trace and Profiler instrument application code.","Cloud Monitoring",["The Cloud Trace library records request latency spans from application code, not OS memory or disk usage.",null,"Cloud Profiler samples CPU and memory use by function in your code, not system-wide memory and disk metrics.","Uptime checks test availability from outside and can't see inside the VM."],1],["ace67",0,3,"You want to be alerted if your public website https://www.example.com stops responding from several locations around the world. What should you configure?",["A log-based metric on the VMs' syslog","A Cloud Trace sampling policy","An uptime check with an alerting policy","Firewall rules logging"],2,"Uptime checks probe a public URL from several global locations, and an alerting policy notifies you when checks fail. Logs and traces only see requests that reach your app.","Cloud Monitoring",["A log-based metric sees only what the VMs log; if the site is down or unreachable, it may produce no logs at all.","Trace sampling controls how requests are traced, not whether the site answers from around the world.",null,"Firewall rules logging records connections allowed or denied by rules; it doesn't test availability from external locations."],2],["ace68",0,3,"Security requires audit logs from every project in the organization to be copied to one BigQuery dataset. What should you create?",["An aggregated sink at the organization level that includes child resources and routes to BigQuery","A sink in each existing project, created by hand, that routes that project's audit logs to the shared dataset","A Monitoring dashboard for the organization","A log-based metric for audit entries"],0,"An organization-level aggregated sink (--include-children) routes matching logs from all projects to one destination, including future projects. Per-project sinks are error-prone and miss new projects.","Cloud Logging",[null,"Per-project sinks must be created by hand and miss any project added later.","A dashboard displays metrics; it doesn't copy log entries anywhere.","A log-based metric counts matching entries but doesn't store the entries in BigQuery."],3],["ace69",0,3,"You need to count how often the text payment declined appears in application logs and chart it over time. What should you create?",["An uptime check","A log bucket with longer retention","A Pub/Sub subscription","A counter log-based metric with a filter for that text"],3,"A counter log-based metric turns matching log entries into a metric that you can chart and alert on. Retention and Pub/Sub don't produce a metric.","Cloud Logging",["An uptime check probes endpoints from outside; it doesn't read log text.","Longer retention keeps logs longer but doesn't turn matching entries into a chartable metric.","A Pub/Sub subscription delivers messages; counting and charting would still require extra tooling.",null],2],["ace70",0,3,"An auditor asks who deleted a Compute Engine VM last week. Where should you look?",["Data Access audit logs, after enabling them","Admin Activity audit logs in Cloud Logging","VPC Flow Logs for the subnet","Cloud Trace spans"],1,"Admin Activity audit logs record calls that change configuration, such as deleting a VM, and are always on. Data Access logs record reads and would need to have been enabled already. Flow logs show network traffic.","Cloud Audit Logs",["Deleting a VM is a configuration change, which Admin Activity logs record; Data Access logs cover reads and aren't needed here.",null,"Flow logs record network traffic samples, not API calls that deleted a VM.","Trace spans show request latency inside applications, not who called the Compute Engine API."],2],["ace71",0,3,"You must record which users read objects in a sensitive Cloud Storage bucket. What must you do?",["Nothing, because Admin Activity logs already record object reads","Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the subnet","Enable Data Access audit logs (DATA_READ) for Cloud Storage","Turn on Object Versioning"],2,"Reading object data is recorded in Data Access audit logs, which are off by default for Cloud Storage. Admin Activity logs cover configuration changes only, and flow logs don't show identities.","Cloud Audit Logs",["Admin Activity logs cover configuration changes, not reads of object data.","Flow logs record network flows and don't show which identity read which object.",null,"Versioning keeps old object versions and records nothing about who read an object."],3],["ace72",0,3,"A Python app on Cloud Run throws occasional exceptions. You want them grouped, counted and alerted on with no extra infrastructure. Which service fits?",["Error Reporting","Cloud Profiler","Cloud Trace","Cloud Scheduler"],0,"Error Reporting groups similar exceptions from logs, counts them and can notify you of new errors. Profiler measures CPU and memory use, and Trace measures request latency.","Troubleshooting tools",[null,"Cloud Profiler shows CPU and memory usage by function, not grouped exceptions with alerts.","Cloud Trace shows request latency across services; it doesn't group or count exceptions.","Cloud Scheduler runs jobs on a schedule and has nothing to do with collecting errors."],2],["ace73",0,4,"A developer needs to start and stop VMs in one project but must not delete them or change IAM. The Editor role is too broad. What should you do?",["Create a custom role with compute.instances.start, stop, get and list, and grant it on the project","Grant the basic Owner role on the project","Grant the basic Viewer role on the project","Grant the predefined Compute Instance Admin (v1) role on the project, since it covers every instance action"],0,"A custom role with only the needed permissions gives least privilege. Owner and Compute Instance Admin also allow deleting VMs (and Owner allows IAM changes), and Viewer can't start or stop VMs.","IAM roles",[null,"Owner allows deleting VMs and changing IAM, the two things the developer must not be able to do.","Viewer is read-only, so the developer couldn't start or stop VMs.","Compute Instance Admin also includes deleting instances, which the developer must not be able to do."],3],["ace74",0,4,"Which statement about the basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) is correct?",["Google recommends them for production because they're simple","Editor includes the permissions needed to view and change the project's IAM allow policy","They're very broad, so predefined or custom roles are preferred for least privilege","They can only be granted at the organization level"],2,"Basic roles include thousands of permissions across services, so Google recommends predefined or custom roles instead. Editor can't manage IAM policies (Owner can), and basic roles can be granted on projects.","IAM roles",["Google recommends predefined or custom roles instead, because basic roles grant far more than most jobs need.","Editor can't view or change IAM policies; that requires Owner or an IAM admin role.",null,"Basic roles can be granted on projects (and other levels), not only the organization."],2],["ace75",0,4,"An app on a Compute Engine VM needs to write objects to one Cloud Storage bucket. What is the recommended way to give it access?",["Create a service account key and copy the JSON file onto the VM","Attach a dedicated service account to the VM and grant it Storage Object Creator on that bucket","Keep the default Compute Engine service account and its Editor role, since Editor already includes Storage access","Run gcloud auth login on the VM with a developer's account"],1,"An attached, dedicated service account gets short-lived credentials from the metadata server, and a bucket-level role limits it to what it needs. Keys can leak, Editor is far too broad, and user credentials tie the app to a person.","Service accounts",["Key files are long-lived secrets that can leak; an attached service account gets short-lived tokens with no key to manage.",null,"Editor does include Storage access, but it also allows changes to almost everything in the project, which breaks least privilege.","User credentials tie the application to a person and grant everything that developer can do."],2],["ace76",0,4,"A CI pipeline on GitHub Actions needs to deploy to Google Cloud, and security forbids long-lived service account keys. What should you use?",["A service account key stored as a GitHub secret","An API key restricted to the deployment APIs","A personal user account shared with the pipeline","Workload Identity Federation with GitHub's OIDC tokens, impersonating a deploy service account"],3,"Workload Identity Federation exchanges the pipeline's OIDC token for short-lived Google credentials, so no key exists. API keys don't authorize most admin APIs, and shared personal accounts break accountability.","Workload Identity Federation",["A key stored as a secret is still a long-lived key, which the security team forbids.","API keys identify a project for some public APIs but can't authorize deployment actions as an IAM principal.","Sharing a personal account breaks accountability and puts a user's full access in the pipeline.",null],3],["ace77",0,4,"Pods in a GKE cluster must call Cloud Storage as a specific identity, without relying on the node's service account or on keys. What should you use?",["Workload Identity Federation for GKE, granting IAM roles to the pod's Kubernetes service account","Grant the node's service account the Storage Admin role","Create a service account key, store it in a Kubernetes Secret and mount it into the pods as a file","The basic Editor role on the node's service account"],0,"Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets a Kubernetes service account act as an IAM principal (or impersonate a Google service account), so each workload gets only its own access. Node-level roles are shared by every pod on the node.","Workload Identity Federation",[null,"A role on the node's service account is shared by every pod on the node and relies on the node identity, which the requirement rules out.","A key mounted as a Secret is still a long-lived key, which the requirement rules out.","Editor on the node's service account is far too broad and still shares one identity among all pods."],3],["ace78",0,4,"An administrator wants to run gcloud commands as the service account deploy-sa to test its permissions, without creating a key. What should they be granted and use?",["Service Account Key Admin, then create and download a key","Owner on the project, then download the service account's credentials and sign in as the service account","Service Account Token Creator on deploy-sa, then run gcloud with --impersonate-service-account","Viewer on deploy-sa, then use gcloud auth login"],2,"Token Creator lets a principal get short-lived tokens for the service account, which gcloud uses with --impersonate-service-account. No key is created, and each call is logged with both identities.","Service account impersonation",["Creating and downloading a key is exactly what the requirement forbids.","Owner is far too broad, and downloading credentials means creating a key, which isn't allowed.",null,"Viewer on the service account doesn't allow generating tokens for it, and gcloud auth login signs in as a user, not a service account."],3],["ace79",0,4,"A user can create VMs but gets a permission error when attaching the service account app-sa to a new VM. What do they need on app-sa?",["Compute Viewer","Service Account User (roles/iam.serviceAccountUser)","Security Reviewer","Logs Writer"],1,"Attaching a service account lets the VM act as it, so Google requires Service Account User on that account. The other roles don't include iam.serviceAccounts.actAs.","Service accounts",["Compute Viewer is read-only for Compute Engine and doesn't include permission to act as a service account.",null,"Security Reviewer can view IAM policies but can't attach or act as a service account.","Logs Writer only lets an identity write log entries; it doesn't allow using a service account."],3],["ace80",0,4,"Staff must reach an internal web app on Compute Engine over HTTPS based on their identity, without a VPN. What should you use?",["Cloud NAT on the app's subnet","Firewall rules that allow each user's home IP","VPC Network Peering with each user's network","Identity-Aware Proxy on the app's HTTPS load balancer backend"],3,"IAP checks each request's user identity (and optional context) before it reaches the app. IP allowlists are fragile and identity-blind, and Cloud NAT is outbound only.","Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login",["Cloud NAT provides outbound internet access for private VMs, not identity-based inbound access for users.","IP allowlists don't check who the user is and break whenever someone's home address changes.","Peering connects VPC networks, not individual users' home networks, and doesn't check identity.",null],2],["ace81",0,4,"Linux SSH access to VMs must be controlled by IAM roles and removed automatically when an employee's account is disabled. What should you enable?",["Project-wide SSH keys in metadata","Interactive serial console access","OS Login, with roles such as Compute OS Login","Shielded VM secure boot"],2,"OS Login ties Linux accounts to Google identities and IAM, so disabling the account removes access. Metadata SSH keys stay valid until someone removes them.","Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login",["Metadata SSH keys stay valid until someone removes them, so disabling an account doesn't cut off access.","The serial console is a troubleshooting tool, not an IAM-controlled way to manage everyday SSH access.",null,"Secure boot protects the boot process from tampering; it doesn't control who can SSH in."],3],["ace82",0,4,"Compliance requires that you control, rotate and can disable the key protecting a bucket's data, while Google still performs the encryption. What should you use?",["Google default encryption with Google-owned keys","A Cloud KMS key set as the bucket's default customer-managed encryption key (CMEK)","Customer-supplied encryption keys that you generate and send along with every read and write request","Uniform bucket-level access"],1,"With CMEK you manage the key in Cloud KMS (IAM, rotation, disabling) and Google uses it to encrypt data. Default encryption gives you no key control, and customer-supplied keys must be sent with every request.","Encryption",["With default encryption Google controls the keys, so you can't rotate on your own schedule or disable them.",null,"CSEK does give control, but Google doesn't store the keys and you must send them with every request, unlike the managed option described.","Uniform bucket-level access controls IAM permissions; it has nothing to do with encryption keys."],2],["ace83",0,4,"An app on Cloud Run needs a database password. Where should it be kept?",["Hard-coded in the container image","In a plain environment variable defined in the deployment YAML committed to the source repository","In a Cloud Storage object readable by allUsers","In Secret Manager, with Secret Accessor granted to the service's service account"],3,"Secret Manager stores secrets encrypted, versioned and access-controlled by IAM, and Cloud Run can expose them as environment variables or files. The other places leak the password to anyone with the image, repo or URL.","Secret Manager",["Anyone who can pull the image can read the password, and changing it means rebuilding the image.","A password in the repository is readable by everyone with repo access and stays in its history.","allUsers makes the object readable by anyone on the internet.",null],1],["ace84",0,4,"A bucket must use only IAM for access control, with object ACLs disabled so permissions are consistent. What should you enable?",["Fine-grained access control","Requester Pays","Uniform bucket-level access","Object Versioning"],2,"Uniform bucket-level access disables ACLs, so only bucket-level IAM applies. Fine-grained is the mode that allows ACLs.","Cloud Storage access",["Fine-grained access is the mode that keeps ACLs, the opposite of what's required.","Requester Pays changes who pays for access charges, not how permissions are controlled.",null,"Versioning keeps old object versions; it doesn't change the access control model."],1],["ace85",0,4,"A customer with no Google account must download one private object during the next hour. What should you provide?",["A signed URL that expires in one hour","Public access on the whole bucket","Storage Object Viewer granted to allUsers on the bucket","A copy of a service account key"],0,"A signed URL grants time-limited access to one object without a Google identity, and the bucket stays private. Public access exposes everything, and sharing keys is unsafe.","Cloud Storage access",[null,"Making the whole bucket public exposes every object to anyone, forever, not just one file for an hour.","Granting allUsers read access makes every object in the bucket public with no expiry.","Sharing a service account key hands over broad, long-lived access and is a serious security risk."],2],["ace86",0,4,"You must make sure no bucket in the organization can be made public, even by mistake. What should you set?",["A label public=false on every bucket","The organization policy constraint that enforces public access prevention","Uniform bucket-level access turned on for every existing bucket in each project of the organization","Object Versioning on every bucket"],1,"Enforcing public access prevention through an organization policy blocks allUsers and allAuthenticatedUsers access on all buckets below it. Labels don't enforce anything, and uniform access still allows public IAM grants.","Cloud Storage access",["Labels are metadata for organizing and billing; they don't enforce anything.",null,"Uniform bucket-level access disables ACLs but still allows IAM grants to allUsers, and it doesn't cover new buckets.","Versioning keeps old versions of objects and doesn't block public access."],3],["ace87",0,4,"A user says they can't read a BigQuery dataset, and you want to see which binding is missing or which policy blocks them. Which tool helps?",["Cloud Trace","Error Reporting","The billing cost table","Policy Troubleshooter"],3,"Policy Troubleshooter checks whether a principal has a permission on a resource and shows which policies grant or deny it. The other tools don't evaluate IAM.","Reviewing access",["Cloud Trace analyzes request latency, not IAM permissions.","Error Reporting groups application exceptions; it doesn't evaluate IAM bindings.","The cost table shows billing data, not who has which permissions.",null],3],["ace88",0,4,"You want to find principals with more permissions than they have used recently and get suggestions for smaller roles. What should you use?",["Budget alerts","VPC Flow Logs","IAM role recommendations from the IAM recommender","Cloud Monitoring uptime checks"],2,"The IAM recommender compares granted permissions with actual usage and suggests removing or replacing roles. The other tools track spend, traffic or availability.","Reviewing access",["Budget alerts track spending, not which permissions principals use.","Flow logs record network traffic, not IAM permission usage.",null,"Uptime checks test availability of endpoints and say nothing about IAM roles."],2],["ace89",0,4,"A service account key was accidentally committed to a public repository. What should you do first?",["Disable and delete the key, then review audit logs for activity by the service account","Rewrite the repository history to remove the commit containing the key, then force-push the cleaned branch","Rename the service account","Change the project's display name"],0,"The key stays valid until you disable or delete it, and copies may already exist, so revoke it first and then look for misuse. Removing the commit doesn't invalidate the key.","Service account keys",[null,"Rewriting history doesn't invalidate the key, and anyone who already copied it can still use it.","Renaming isn't possible for a service account's email, and even a new display name leaves the key valid.","The project's display name has nothing to do with whether the leaked key works."],2],["ace90",0,4,"An external auditor must be able to list resources and view IAM policies across the organization, without being able to change anything. Which role fits?",["Organization Administrator","Owner on every project","Editor at the organization","Security Reviewer at the organization"],3,"Security Reviewer grants permissions to list resources and read IAM policies without making changes. The other roles can modify resources or access.","Reviewing access",["Organization Administrator can manage IAM policies across the organization, which is far more than viewing.","Owner allows full changes to every project, not read-only review.","Editor can modify almost every resource, so the auditor could change things.",null],3],["x1",0,1,"A developer shut down (deleted) the project reports-dev by mistake two days ago. What is the BEST way to get it back?",["Run gcloud projects undelete during the recovery window","Create a new project with the same project ID","Open a billing support case to recreate the project from backups","Restore the project from the most recent Cloud Storage snapshot"],0,"A shut-down project stays pending deletion for about 30 days and can be restored by an authorized user during that window. Recreating it with the same ID is tempting, but project IDs are never reused and the resources would be missing.","Domain 1: Projects",[null,"A deleted project's ID cannot be reused, and a new project would not contain the original resources.","Billing support does not rebuild projects; the owner can restore it directly during the recovery window.","Projects are not backed up as Cloud Storage snapshots, so there is nothing to restore that way."],2],["x2",0,1,"A script fails because the Cloud Run Admin API is disabled in project web-prod. Which command enables it?",["gcloud components install run","gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com --project=web-prod","gcloud run deploy --enable-api --project=web-prod --region=us-central1","gcloud config set api/run enabled"],1,"APIs are enabled per project with gcloud services enable followed by the service name. Installing a CLI component is the tempting distractor, but it only changes the local tools.","Domain 1: Enabling APIs",["gcloud components install adds CLI components to your machine and does not enable APIs in a project.",null,"gcloud run deploy has no flag that enables the API, and it would still fail while the API is off.","There is no api/run configuration property; gcloud config stores CLI settings, not project API states."],1],["x3",0,1,"Security wants to stop anyone from creating Compute Engine VMs with external IP addresses in the Production folder, except for a few approved bastion VMs. What should you use?",["A firewall rule that denies all egress traffic in each VPC","A billing budget with an alert at 100%","The compute.vmExternalIpAccess org policy on the folder","An IAM role that removes compute.instances.create from all users"],2,"The vmExternalIpAccess list constraint defines which VMs, if any, may have external IPs and is inherited by projects in the folder. Removing create permissions is tempting but blocks far more than intended.","Domain 1: Organization policies",["Egress firewall rules control traffic flows, not whether a VM can be created with an external IP.","Budgets report on spending and do not block any resource configuration.",null,"Removing VM creation permission blocks all VMs, not just ones with external IPs."],2],["x4",0,1,"You want an IAM condition that grants a role only on resources marked as environment=dev, and the marking must be centrally controlled so project users cannot change it freely. What should you attach to resources?",["Labels that each project's developers apply to their own resources","Network tags on the VMs","A naming convention in each resource name","Resource Manager tags with values defined at the organization"],3,"Resource Manager tags have centrally managed keys and values, their use is controlled by IAM, and IAM conditions can reference them. Labels look similar and are tempting, but they cannot drive IAM conditions.","Domain 1: Tags and labels",["Labels are for organizing and billing and are not supported as the basis for IAM conditions.","Network tags only target firewall rules and routes, not IAM policy decisions.","Names can be chosen by anyone who creates a resource, so they are not a controlled access boundary.",null],3],["x5",0,1,"A database VM runs 24/7 at a steady size and will be needed for at least three more years. How can you MOST reduce its compute cost?",["Purchase a 3-year committed use discount for its vCPUs and memory","Convert it to a Spot VM","Move it to a region with the most zones","Stop it every night and start it again every morning before business hours"],0,"Committed use discounts give large savings in exchange for a 1- or 3-year commitment, ideal for steady, predictable workloads. Spot VMs are cheaper still, but they can be stopped at any moment.","Domain 1: Pricing and discounts",[null,"Spot VMs can be preempted at any time, which is unacceptable for an always-on database.","The number of zones in a region does not lower the VM's price.","The database must run 24/7, so nightly stops would cause outages."],2],["x6",0,1,"A team ran an N2 VM for the whole month without buying any commitment, and the bill shows a discount on it. What explains this?",["A free trial credit applied automatically to the VM","Automatic sustained use discounts for running most of the month","A committed use discount purchased by default for every new project","A discount for choosing a zone with low traffic"],1,"Sustained use discounts apply automatically to eligible machine types that run for a significant part of the billing month. Committed use discounts are the tempting answer, but they require an explicit purchase.","Domain 1: Pricing and discounts",["Free trial credits reduce the balance owed but appear as credits, not as a usage-based VM discount.",null,"Committed use discounts must be purchased; they are never applied by default.","Google Cloud does not discount VMs based on how busy a zone is."],2],["x7",0,1,"Before deploying anything, a manager wants a monthly cost estimate for four VMs, a Cloud SQL instance and 2 TB of Cloud Storage. Which tool should you use?",["Cloud Monitoring dashboards","The billing export to BigQuery","The Google Cloud Pricing Calculator","Cloud Asset Inventory"],2,"The Pricing Calculator estimates costs for planned configurations before anything is created. The billing export is tempting for cost analysis, but it only contains actual past charges.","Domain 1: Cost planning",["Monitoring shows metrics for resources that already exist, not prices for planned ones.","Billing export contains costs that were already incurred, so it is empty for resources not yet deployed.",null,"Asset Inventory lists existing resources and their metadata, not estimated prices."],1],["x8",0,1,"You need your gcloud commands to run against the project analytics-prod by default. Which command sets this?",["gcloud projects create analytics-prod","gcloud init --project-only","gcloud auth login analytics-prod","gcloud config set project analytics-prod"],3,"gcloud config set project stores the default project in the active configuration. Creating the project is the tempting distractor, but it does not make it the default.","Domain 1: Cloud SDK configuration",["gcloud projects create makes a new project; it does not change which project commands use.","gcloud init has no --project-only flag; it runs the full interactive setup.","gcloud auth login signs in a user account and does not take a project ID.",null],1],["x9",0,1,"A company does not use Google Workspace but wants to create and manage user accounts and groups centrally for Google Cloud access at no extra license cost. What should it use?",["Cloud Identity Free edition","Personal Gmail accounts for each employee","Service accounts for every employee","Workforce accounts created in each project"],0,"Cloud Identity provides managed user and group accounts without Workspace, and its Free edition covers basic identity needs. Personal Gmail accounts are tempting because they are quick, but the company loses control of them.","Domain 1: Identity setup",[null,"Personal Gmail accounts are owned by the individuals, so the company cannot centrally manage or remove them.","Service accounts represent workloads, not people, and should not be used as employee identities.","Projects do not hold user accounts; identities are managed at the organization's identity provider."],2],["x10",0,1,"Which practice is recommended for Cloud Identity or Workspace super administrator accounts?",["Use the super admin account for everyday Google Cloud work","Enforce strong MFA and use them only when truly needed","Share one super admin account among the whole IT team","Grant super admin to every project owner"],1,"Super admin accounts are extremely powerful, so they should have strong MFA, be few in number and be used only when necessary. Using one for daily work is convenient but greatly increases risk.","Domain 1: Identity setup",["Daily use increases the chance that the most powerful account is phished or misused.",null,"Shared accounts remove accountability and make the credentials harder to protect.","Project owners do not need identity-wide administrator rights, which violates least privilege."],2],["x11",0,1,"An organization policy on the Production folder restricts resource locations to Europe. One project in that folder must also use a US region for a research workload. What should you do?",["Move every other project out of the Production folder","Delete the organization policy from the folder","Set a project-level policy that overrides the inherited one","Grant the project's users the Owner role so the policy no longer applies"],2,"Organization policies are inherited, but a policy set on a lower level such as a project can override the parent for that resource. Granting Owner is tempting, but org policies apply regardless of IAM permissions.","Domain 1: Organization policies",["Moving all the other projects is disruptive and unnecessary for a single exception.","Deleting the folder policy removes the restriction for every project, not just the one that needs it.",null,"IAM roles, even Owner, do not bypass organization policy constraints."],3],["x12",0,1,"Security and billing notifications from Google Cloud keep going to a former employee. How should you make sure the right teams receive them?",["Create a Cloud Monitoring uptime check for the security team","Add the teams as Viewers on every project","Create a billing budget for each team","Set up Essential Contacts for security and billing"],3,"Essential Contacts lets you name who receives Google Cloud notifications by category, such as security, billing and technical. Budgets are tempting for the billing part, but they do not cover security notices.","Domain 1: Notifications",["Uptime checks monitor endpoints and have nothing to do with Google's account notifications.","Viewer access lets people see resources but does not route Google's notifications to them.","Budgets send cost alerts only, not security or other administrative notifications.",null],3],["x13",0,1,"A sandbox project must automatically stop incurring charges when its spend reaches its budget. What should you build?",["Pub/Sub budget notifications that trigger a function to disable billing","A budget with an email alert at 100% sent to the project owner and the finance team","A quota limit on the number of VMs in the project","A label named max-spend on the project"],0,"Budgets can publish programmatic notifications to Pub/Sub, and a subscriber function can respond automatically, for example by disabling billing. An email alert is tempting, but budgets alone never cap spending.","Domain 1: Budgets",[null,"An email alert only informs someone; spending continues until a person acts.","VM quotas limit one resource type and do not stop charges from other services.","Labels are metadata for reporting and do not enforce any spending limit."],3],["x14",0,1,"Which statement about Cloud Billing accounts and projects is correct?",["A project can be linked to several billing accounts at the same time to split its costs","Each project uses one billing account; one account can pay for many projects","Every project must have its own dedicated billing account","Billing accounts can only be linked to projects in the same folder"],1,"Each project uses a single billing account at a time, and a billing account commonly pays for many projects. Needing one billing account per project is a tempting misconception, but sharing is normal.","Domain 1: Billing accounts",["A project can only be linked to one billing account at a time, though it can be moved to another.",null,"Many projects can share one billing account, so a dedicated account per project is not required.","Billing account links are not limited by folder; projects across the organization can share an account."],2],["x15",0,2,"A Python web app uses a supported runtime, gets unpredictable bursts of traffic and should scale to zero when idle. The team prefers a platform-as-a-service with no containers to build. Which option fits BEST?",["App Engine flexible environment","A regional managed instance group","App Engine standard environment","GKE Standard with a fixed node pool"],2,"App Engine standard runs code in supported runtimes, scales quickly and can scale to zero instances when idle. The flexible environment is tempting because it is also App Engine, but it always keeps at least one instance.","Domain 2: App Engine",["The flexible environment runs on VMs, keeps at least one instance running and scales more slowly.","A managed instance group needs VM images and templates and does not scale to zero on its own.",null,"A fixed node pool keeps nodes running and requires container images and cluster management."],2],["x16",0,2,"A partner system will call a small piece of Node.js code over HTTPS a few hundred times a day to record a webhook event. You want to deploy just the function code with no servers to manage. What should you use?",["A Compute Engine VM running a web server","A GKE Autopilot cluster","Cloud Storage static website hosting","Cloud Run functions with an HTTP trigger"],3,"Cloud Run functions deploy individual functions from source with an HTTP trigger and scale automatically with no servers to manage. GKE Autopilot is tempting because it is managed, but it is still a cluster built for containers.","Domain 2: Serverless compute",["A VM runs and costs money all day and must be patched, which is excessive for a small webhook.","A GKE cluster is far more infrastructure than a single small function needs.","Static website hosting serves files and cannot run server-side code for each request.",null],1],["x17",0,2,"You are creating a managed instance group for a web tier. What must exist before you can create the group?",["An instance template describing the VM configuration","A Cloud SQL instance that the web tier will use for session data","An external HTTPS load balancer","A custom machine type"],0,"A managed instance group builds identical VMs from an instance template, so the template must exist first. A load balancer is a common companion, which makes it tempting, but it is optional.","Domain 2: Managed instance groups",[null,"A MIG does not need a database; it only needs to know how to build its VMs.","A load balancer is often added in front of a MIG, but the group can be created without one.","Any machine type, predefined or custom, can be set in the template; a custom type is not required."],1],["x18",0,2,"A web tier running in a zonal managed instance group went down completely during a zone outage. How should you redesign it so the group survives the loss of one zone?",["Increase the zonal group's maximum number of instances","A regional managed instance group across zones","Add a second network interface to each VM","Switch the VMs to a larger machine type"],1,"A regional MIG distributes instances across multiple zones in a region, so losing one zone leaves the others serving. Raising the instance count is tempting, but it keeps every VM in the single failed zone.","Domain 2: High availability",["More instances in the same zone still fail together when that zone goes down.",null,"Extra network interfaces do not move VMs out of the failed zone.","Larger machines in one zone are just as affected by a zone outage."],2],["x19",0,2,"Application VMs must reach a pool of database proxy VMs over TCP port 6432 using a single internal IP address in the same region, and the proxies must see the original client IP. Which load balancer fits?",["Global external Application Load Balancer","External proxy Network Load Balancer","Internal passthrough Network Load Balancer","Cloud CDN"],2,"An internal passthrough Network Load Balancer gives a regional internal IP for TCP or UDP and passes packets through, preserving the client's source IP. A proxy load balancer is tempting for TCP, but it is external and terminates the connection.","Domain 2: Load balancing",["An external Application Load Balancer is internet-facing and built for HTTP(S), not internal TCP traffic.","An external proxy load balancer faces the internet and terminates connections, so backends do not see the client IP directly.",null,"Cloud CDN caches HTTP content at the edge and does not balance internal TCP connections."],3],["x20",0,2,"A mobile app stores user profiles and notes as documents and must keep working offline, syncing changes when the device reconnects. Which database fits BEST?",["Bigtable","BigQuery","Cloud SQL for MySQL","Firestore"],3,"Firestore is a serverless document database with mobile and web SDKs that support offline data and real-time sync. Cloud SQL is tempting as a familiar managed database, but it lacks built-in offline sync.","Domain 2: Choosing a database",["Bigtable is built for massive analytical and time-series workloads and has no mobile offline sync.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not an operational database for app users.","Cloud SQL is relational and does not provide client libraries with offline sync for mobile devices.",null],1],["x21",0,2,"Analysts need to run SQL over several terabytes of sales history without managing any servers or indexes. Which service should you use?",["BigQuery","Memorystore","Firestore","Filestore"],0,"BigQuery is a serverless data warehouse that runs SQL over very large datasets with no infrastructure to manage. Firestore is tempting as another serverless database, but it is built for app transactions.","Domain 2: Choosing a data service",[null,"Memorystore is an in-memory cache for fast key lookups, not a SQL analytics engine.","Firestore is an operational document database and is not designed for large analytical SQL scans.","Filestore provides managed NFS file shares, not a query engine."],1],["x22",0,2,"A web app repeatedly reads the same product details from Cloud SQL, and the database is under heavy load. You want a managed in-memory cache in front of it. Which service fits?",["Cloud Storage","Memorystore for Redis","Bigtable","Persistent Disk"],1,"Memorystore provides managed Redis or Memcached for sub-millisecond caching that offloads repeated reads. Bigtable is tempting because it is fast, but it is a full database rather than a cache.","Domain 2: Caching",["Cloud Storage is object storage with higher latency than an in-memory cache.",null,"Bigtable is a wide-column database for huge workloads, not a lightweight cache.","Persistent Disk is block storage attached to VMs, not a shared in-memory cache."],1],["x23",0,2,"Monthly reports are uploaded to a bucket and read about once a month for a quarter, then rarely. Which storage class is the BEST fit for new objects to minimize cost?",["Standard","Archive","Nearline","Coldline"],2,"Nearline is designed for data accessed about once a month or less, with a 30-day minimum storage duration. Coldline is tempting because it is cheaper to store, but its retrieval costs and 90-day minimum suit less frequent access.","Domain 2: Cloud Storage classes",["Standard has the highest storage price and is intended for data accessed frequently.","Archive has a 365-day minimum storage duration and high retrieval costs, poor for monthly reads.",null,"Coldline targets data accessed less than once a quarter, so monthly reads would add retrieval charges."],1],["x24",0,2,"An order service must hand off events to billing, email and analytics services without waiting for them, and those services may be temporarily down. Which service should connect them?",["Cloud DNS","Cloud Scheduler","Cloud Storage Transfer Service","Pub/Sub"],3,"Pub/Sub decouples publishers from subscribers, stores messages until each subscriber acknowledges them and fans out one event to many consumers. Cloud Scheduler is tempting for triggering work, but it runs on a clock, not on events.","Domain 2: Messaging",["Cloud DNS resolves names and does not deliver or buffer messages.","Cloud Scheduler triggers jobs on a timetable, not in response to each order event.","Storage Transfer Service moves files between storage systems, not application messages.",null],1],["x25",0,2,"Clickstream events arrive continuously in Pub/Sub and must be cleaned, windowed and aggregated before being written to BigQuery, using a managed service that autoscales. What should you use?",["Dataflow running an Apache Beam streaming pipeline","Cloud Scheduler running a query every minute","A single Compute Engine VM running a cron job with a Python script","Cloud Storage Object Lifecycle Management"],0,"Dataflow runs Apache Beam pipelines for streaming and batch data with autoscaling and built-in windowing. A cron job on a VM is tempting because it is simple, but it neither autoscales nor processes streams natively.","Domain 2: Data processing",[null,"Scheduled queries run in batches and cannot perform continuous, windowed stream processing.","A single VM must be managed and scaled by hand and is a single point of failure.","Lifecycle rules change storage classes and delete objects; they do not process data."],2],["x26",0,2,"VPC-A is peered with VPC-B, and VPC-B is peered with VPC-C. VMs in VPC-A cannot reach VMs in VPC-C. Why?",["The VMs in VPC-C are in a different region","Peering is not transitive; A and C need their own peering","Firewall rules never apply to peered networks","Peering only works between VPC networks that belong to the same project"],1,"Peering routes are not passed on through a middle network, so A reaches B and B reaches C, but A cannot reach C without a direct peering or another design. Blaming regions is tempting, but VPCs and peering are global.","Domain 2: VPC peering",["VPC networks are global, and peering works across regions, so region is not the cause.",null,"Firewall rules do apply to traffic between peered networks, but they are not why routes are missing here.","Peering works across projects and even organizations, so the project is not the limitation."],3],["x27",0,2,"VMs with only internal IP addresses need to read objects from Cloud Storage and call other Google APIs, without any internet access for other destinations. What should you enable?",["An external IP address on each VM","A VPN tunnel to the on-premises network","Private Google Access on the VMs' subnet","Cloud CDN on the bucket"],2,"Private Google Access, enabled per subnet, lets VMs without external IPs reach Google APIs and services. Adding external IPs is tempting because it works, but it exposes the VMs to the internet.","Domain 2: Private access",["External IPs would expose the VMs and give them general internet access, which is not wanted.","A VPN connects to on-premises networks and does not by itself give access to Google APIs.",null,"Cloud CDN caches content for end users and does not let internal VMs reach Google APIs."],2],["x28",0,2,"A VPC has a firewall rule that denies TCP 22 from 0.0.0.0/0 at priority 1000 and another that allows TCP 22 from 35.235.240.0/20 at priority 900. What happens to SSH traffic from 35.235.240.10?",["It is denied, because deny rules always override allow rules","It is denied, because the broader rule applies first","It is allowed only if the rules were created in the right order","It is allowed, because priority 900 is evaluated first"],3,"Lower priority numbers take precedence, so the allow rule at 900 matches before the deny at 1000. Assuming deny always wins is tempting, but that only applies when two rules have the same priority.","Domain 2: VPC firewall rules",["Google Cloud firewall rules are evaluated by priority, not by whether they allow or deny.","Rule breadth does not decide the order; the priority number does.","Creation order has no effect on how firewall rules are evaluated.",null],3],["x29",0,2,"Internal services should resolve names such as db.corp.internal to private IPs, and those names must not be visible on the internet. What should you create in Cloud DNS?",["A private zone authorized for the VPC networks that need it","A public zone with DNSSEC turned on","A forwarding rule on an external load balancer that points to the database VMs","A Cloud CDN cache key for the domain"],0,"Private zones are only visible to the VPC networks you authorize, so internal names resolve privately. A public zone with DNSSEC is tempting because it sounds secure, but DNSSEC protects integrity, not privacy.","Domain 2: Cloud DNS",[null,"A public zone is resolvable from the internet, even with DNSSEC, which exposes internal names.","Load balancer forwarding rules route traffic and do not answer DNS queries.","CDN cache keys control caching behavior and have nothing to do with name resolution."],2],["x30",0,2,"Several engineers apply Terraform to the same Google Cloud environment from their laptops, and state files keep getting out of sync. What should the team do?",["Email the terraform.tfstate file to each other after every apply","Keep state in a Cloud Storage bucket via the gcs backend","Delete the state file before each apply so Terraform starts fresh","Commit the state file to the Git repository alongside the code"],1,"A shared remote backend in Cloud Storage gives everyone the same state and supports locking to prevent concurrent changes. Committing state to Git is tempting because the code is there, but it lacks locking and may expose secrets.","Domain 2: Infrastructure as code",["Emailing state is manual, error-prone and gives no locking against simultaneous applies.",null,"Without state, Terraform loses track of existing resources and may try to recreate or orphan them.","State in Git can contain secrets and does not lock against two people applying at once."],3],["x31",0,2,"A VM's 200 GB data persistent disk is almost full, and the app cannot be taken offline. What should you do?",["Stop the VM, detach the disk and recreate it with a larger size","Attach a Local SSD and move the database files onto it","Grow the disk while the VM runs, then extend the file system","Create a snapshot and restore it to a smaller disk"],2,"Persistent disks can be enlarged without stopping the VM; afterward, the partition and file system are extended inside the guest. Stopping and recreating is tempting as a safe path, but it causes avoidable downtime.","Domain 2: Persistent Disk",["Persistent disks can be resized online, so stopping the VM causes unnecessary downtime.","Local SSD data is lost when the VM stops, which is risky for database files.",null,"Restoring to a smaller disk would make the space problem worse, and disks cannot be shrunk."],2],["x32",0,2,"A licensed application's terms require it to run on physical servers dedicated to your company, not shared with other customers. Which Compute Engine feature meets this?",["Shielded VMs","Spot VMs","Confidential VMs","Sole-tenant nodes"],3,"Sole-tenant nodes are physical servers dedicated to one project's VMs, which helps meet licensing or compliance requirements. Confidential VMs are tempting because they add isolation, but they still share hardware with other customers.","Domain 2: Compute Engine options",["Shielded VMs protect boot integrity but still run on shared physical hosts.","Spot VMs use spare shared capacity and can be stopped at any time.","Confidential VMs encrypt memory in use but do not dedicate the physical host to you.",null],3],["x33",0,3,"To save money, a team stops a development VM every evening. Which charges continue while the VM is stopped?",["Its persistent disks and any reserved static external IP address","Its vCPUs and memory at the full hourly rate","Nothing, because a stopped VM is completely free until it is started again","Only the network egress it used during the day"],0,"Stopping a VM ends vCPU and memory charges, but its persistent disks and reserved static IP addresses are still billed. Thinking a stopped VM is free is tempting, but storage keeps costing money.","Domain 3: Managing Compute Engine",[null,"Stopped VMs are not billed for vCPUs and memory.","Storage attached to a stopped VM is still billed, so it is not completely free.","Egress is charged when it happens, not as an ongoing charge while the VM is stopped."],2],["x34",0,3,"A managed instance group of 10 VMs must move to a new instance template with no drop in serving capacity during the change. What should you do?",["Delete the group and recreate it with the new template","Run a rolling update to the new template with maxUnavailable 0 and surge","Set the new template on the group, then manually reboot every VM in the group","Edit each VM's settings in place to match the new template"],1,"A rolling update replaces VMs with ones built from the new template, and maxUnavailable 0 with a surge adds new VMs before old ones are removed. Setting the template and rebooting is tempting, but reboots do not rebuild VMs from the new template.","Domain 3: Managed instance groups",["Deleting and recreating the group causes a full outage while the new VMs are built.",null,"Rebooting does not apply a new template to existing VMs, so they would keep the old configuration.","Hand-editing managed VMs drifts from the template and can be undone when the group recreates them."],3],["x35",0,3,"You need to change a standalone VM from e2-standard-2 to e2-standard-8. What must you do first?",["Delete the VM's boot disk","Take a snapshot of every attached disk","Stop the VM","Move the VM to a different zone"],2,"A VM's machine type can only be changed while it is stopped; after the change, you start it again. Taking snapshots first is a sensible habit, which makes it tempting, but it is not the required step.","Domain 3: Managing Compute Engine",["Deleting the boot disk would destroy the operating system and is never needed to resize a VM.","A snapshot is a good precaution but is not required to change the machine type.",null,"The machine type can be changed in the same zone; moving zones is unnecessary."],1],["x36",0,3,"A pod named checkout-7d9f in GKE keeps restarting, and you want to see what the application printed before it crashed. Which command helps MOST directly?",["kubectl get nodes","kubectl scale deployment checkout --replicas=0","kubectl delete pod checkout-7d9f","kubectl logs checkout-7d9f --previous"],3,"kubectl logs with --previous shows the output of the last terminated container, which usually reveals why it crashed. Deleting the pod is a tempting quick fix, but it destroys the evidence.","Domain 3: Managing GKE",["Listing nodes shows node status, not the application's output.","Scaling to zero stops the app entirely and removes the evidence you need.","Deleting the pod replaces it and discards the logs from the crashed container.",null],1],["x37",0,3,"A company wants its GKE clusters to receive tested version upgrades automatically, but only on weekends. What should you configure?",["Use a release channel with a weekend maintenance window","Disable auto-upgrade and upgrade by hand once a year during a quiet period","Create a new cluster for every Kubernetes version","Set the node pool size to zero on weekdays"],0,"Release channels manage automatic, tested upgrades, and maintenance windows limit when they may run. Disabling auto-upgrade is tempting for control, but it leaves clusters behind on security patches.","Domain 3: Managing GKE",[null,"Upgrading once a year leaves clusters on unsupported, unpatched versions for long periods.","Rebuilding clusters for each version is heavy manual work and unnecessary.","Scaling pools to zero stops workloads and does not control when upgrades happen."],2],["x38",0,3,"Each night you must copy only new and changed files from a local directory to gs://backup-bucket/data, leaving unchanged files alone. Which command fits BEST?",["gcloud storage buckets create gs://backup-bucket","gcloud storage rsync ./data gs://backup-bucket/data --recursive","gcloud storage ls gs://backup-bucket/data","gcloud storage rm gs://backup-bucket/data/**"],1,"gcloud storage rsync compares source and destination and transfers only what is new or changed. gcloud storage cp is the usual copy tool, but here rsync avoids re-uploading unchanged files.","Domain 3: Managing Cloud Storage",["Creating a bucket does not copy any files, and the bucket already exists.",null,"Listing objects shows what is there but transfers nothing.","Removing objects deletes the backup instead of updating it."],2],["x39",0,3,"Daily reports query a 20 TB BigQuery events table but only need the last 7 days, and costs are high because every query scans the whole table. What change reduces bytes processed MOST?",["Add LIMIT 1000 to every query","Export the table to Cloud Storage as CSV files","Partition by event date and filter on the partition column","Copy the table to a second dataset in another region and query the copy"],2,"Date partitioning lets BigQuery skip partitions outside the filter, so a 7-day query reads only those partitions. LIMIT is tempting, but it does not reduce the bytes scanned on an unpartitioned table.","Domain 3: Managing BigQuery",["LIMIT reduces the rows returned but, without partition pruning, BigQuery still scans the columns across the whole table.","Querying CSV files in Cloud Storage is usually slower and does not reduce the data read.",null,"A copy in another region doubles storage and still gets fully scanned by each query."],3],["x40",0,3,"At 10:15 someone ran a DELETE without a WHERE clause on a Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL database. Automated backups and point-in-time recovery are enabled. How do you recover the data as of 10:14?",["Promote a read replica, since replicas still hold the rows deleted on the primary","Restore last night's automated backup over the primary","Run the DELETE again with ROLLBACK","Use point-in-time recovery to create a new instance as of 10:14"],3,"Point-in-time recovery restores the database to a specific moment, creating a new instance just before the bad statement. Restoring last night's backup is tempting, but it discards all of this morning's good changes.","Domain 3: Managing Cloud SQL",["Replicas copy changes almost immediately, so the delete has already reached them.","Last night's backup would lose every valid change made between the backup and 10:14.","A committed statement cannot be rolled back afterward, and repeating it does nothing useful.",null],2],["x41",0,3,"Application logs in a project are kept for only 30 days, but compliance requires one year of retention in Cloud Logging. What should you do?",["Raise retention on the log bucket that holds them","Create an uptime check that re-sends the logs every month","Increase the VM's boot disk so it can hold more logs","Enable Data Access audit logs on every service"],0,"Retention is set per log bucket; the _Default bucket keeps logs for 30 days unless you change it, and custom buckets can also be created with longer retention. Enabling more logs is tempting, but it only adds data without keeping it longer.","Domain 3: Cloud Logging",[null,"Uptime checks probe endpoints and do not store or copy logs.","Local disk space does not change how long Cloud Logging retains logs.","Enabling more audit logs increases the log volume but does not change the retention period."],3],["x42",0,3,"A load balancer writes millions of health check request logs a day that nobody uses, and Cloud Logging ingestion costs are rising. What is the BEST way to reduce the cost?",["Delete the load balancer's backend service","Add an exclusion filter for those health check logs","Turn off Cloud Monitoring for the project","Lower the retention of the _Required bucket to one day"],1,"Exclusion filters drop matching log entries before ingestion, so you stop paying for noisy logs you do not need. Changing retention is tempting, but ingestion is charged when logs arrive, regardless of how long they are kept.","Domain 3: Cloud Logging",["Deleting the backend service breaks the application to fix a logging cost.",null,"Cloud Monitoring metrics are separate from log ingestion, so turning it off does not stop these logs.","The _Required bucket's retention cannot be changed and it does not hold these request logs."],3],["x43",0,3,"Users report that some checkout requests take 4 seconds, and a request passes through five microservices on Cloud Run. Which tool BEST shows where the time is spent?",["Cloud Asset Inventory","Error Reporting","Cloud Trace","Cloud Billing reports"],2,"Cloud Trace records distributed traces, showing how long each service call within a request takes so bottlenecks stand out. Error Reporting is tempting for production issues, but slow requests often do not produce errors.","Domain 3: Observability",["Asset Inventory lists resources and their configuration, not request timing.","Error Reporting groups exceptions, but slow requests may not throw any errors.",null,"Billing reports show costs and cannot break down a request's latency."],2],["x44",0,3,"An on-call team wants Cloud Monitoring alerts delivered to its incident-management tool and to a chat channel, not only by email. What should you configure?",["A log sink that exports the alerts to BigQuery","A new VPC firewall rule","A custom IAM role","Notification channels on the alerting policy"],3,"Alerting policies send notifications through notification channels, which include email, SMS, Slack, PagerDuty, webhooks and Pub/Sub. A log sink is tempting because it also routes data, but it moves logs, not alerts.","Domain 3: Cloud Monitoring alerts",["A log sink exports logs for storage or analysis; it does not deliver alert notifications.","Firewall rules control network traffic and have nothing to do with alert delivery.","IAM roles control permissions, not where alert notifications are sent.",null],1],["x45",0,3,"A new Cloud Run revision was sent 100% of traffic and is returning errors. The previous revision, shop-00012, worked fine. What is the fastest way to recover?",["Route 100% of traffic back to revision shop-00012","Delete the Cloud Run service and redeploy it from scratch","Increase the new revision's maximum instances","Change the service's region"],0,"Cloud Run keeps earlier revisions, so traffic can be shifted back to the known-good one immediately. Redeploying is tempting as a clean start, but it is slower and causes downtime.","Domain 3: Managing Cloud Run",[null,"Deleting and redeploying takes longer and causes an outage while the service is rebuilt.","More instances of a broken revision only produce more errors.","Moving regions is slow, disruptive and does not fix the faulty code."],2],["x46",0,3,"A company must move 600 TB from its data center to Cloud Storage, and its internet link would take many months to transfer that much. What should it use?",["gcloud storage cp from a single on-premises server","Transfer Appliance","Cloud VPN with a larger MTU","BigQuery Data Transfer Service"],1,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device you fill on premises and ship to Google, which suits very large datasets on limited bandwidth. A VPN is tempting as a secure transfer path, but it is still limited by the same link.","Domain 3: Data transfer",["Copying over the same slow internet link would still take months.",null,"A VPN runs over the same internet connection, so it does not remove the bandwidth limit.","BigQuery Data Transfer Service schedules loads into BigQuery from supported sources, not bulk file moves from a data center."],2],["x47",0,3,"Security asks for a list of every Cloud Storage bucket and Compute Engine VM across all 120 projects in the organization, including their locations. What is the MOST efficient approach?",["Open each project in the console and write down its resources","Check the billing export for resource names","Search the organization with Cloud Asset Inventory","Run gcloud compute instances list once in any project"],2,"Cloud Asset Inventory can search and export resource metadata across an entire organization in one query. The billing export is tempting because it spans projects, but it is not a resource inventory.","Domain 3: Resource inventory",["Visiting 120 projects by hand is slow and error-prone.","Billing data shows charges by SKU and project, not a complete inventory with resource details.",null,"That command lists VMs in one project only and does not cover buckets."],3],["x48",0,3,"A VM will not finish booting after a kernel update, and SSH does not connect. Which step BEST helps you see what happened during boot?",["Resize the VM's boot disk","Assign the VM a static external IP","Create an uptime check for port 22","View the VM's serial port output"],3,"Serial port output captures kernel and boot messages even when networking and SSH are unavailable. An uptime check is tempting as a diagnostic, but it only confirms the service is unreachable.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting Compute Engine",["A larger disk does not reveal boot errors and rarely fixes a failed kernel update.","A new IP address does not help when the operating system itself is not booting.","An uptime check can confirm SSH is down but cannot show why the VM is failing to boot.",null],2],["x49",0,4,"A support team must be able to view Compute Engine VMs and reset them, but no predefined role grants exactly those permissions without many others. What should you do?",["Create a custom role with just the needed permissions","Grant the Editor basic role on the project to the support group","Grant the Compute Admin role on the organization","Share an administrator's credentials with the team"],0,"A custom role bundles exactly the permissions required and follows least privilege when predefined roles are too broad. Editor is tempting because it works immediately, but it grants far too much.","Domain 4: IAM roles",[null,"Editor allows changes to almost every resource, far beyond viewing and resetting VMs.","Compute Admin at the organization level gives full control of all compute resources everywhere.","Shared credentials destroy accountability and give the team everything the administrator can do."],2],["x50",0,4,"A contractor needs the BigQuery Data Viewer role on one project only until the end of the month, and access must end automatically. What should you use?",["A calendar reminder to remove the binding at the end of the month","An IAM binding with a condition that expires at month end","A service account key that expires at the end of the month","An organization policy that blocks BigQuery after that date"],1,"IAM Conditions can make a binding valid only until a specific date and time, so access ends without manual action. A calendar reminder is tempting because it is simple, but it is not enforced.","Domain 4: IAM Conditions",["A reminder relies on someone remembering, so access can easily outlive the contract.",null,"Giving the contractor a service account key adds a long-lived secret and hides who is acting.","An organization policy that blocks BigQuery would affect everyone, not just the contractor."],3],["x51",0,4,"A reporting service only needs to read objects in one Cloud Storage bucket. Which role should you grant its service account on that bucket?",["Storage Admin (roles/storage.admin)","Owner (roles/owner)","Storage Object Viewer (roles/storage.objectViewer)","Storage Object Creator (roles/storage.objectCreator)"],2,"Storage Object Viewer grants read access to objects, matching the need exactly and following least privilege. Object Creator is tempting because it sounds object-specific, but it only allows writes.","Domain 4: Least privilege",["Storage Admin grants full control of buckets and objects, far more than read access.","Owner grants full control of the whole project, which is far too broad.",null,"Object Creator allows uploading objects but not reading them."],1],["x52",0,4,"New VMs in an older project are created with the Compute Engine default service account, which was granted the Editor role on the project. What is the recommended practice?",["Keep using the default service account because it is managed by Google","Grant the default service account the Owner role so it never fails","Remove all service accounts so VMs cannot call any API","Use a dedicated service account per workload with minimal roles"],3,"User-managed service accounts with narrowly scoped roles limit what a compromised VM can do. Keeping the default account is tempting for convenience, but it can carry the broad Editor role.","Domain 4: Service accounts",["Being Google-created does not make the default account safe; its broad Editor grant is the problem.","Adding Owner makes the over-privileged account even more dangerous.","Workloads usually need some API access, so removing every service account breaks them.",null],2],["x53",0,4,"A VM's service account has the Storage Object Admin role on a bucket, yet uploads from the VM fail with an 'insufficient authentication scopes' error. What is the MOST likely cause?",["The VM's access scopes only allow read-only Cloud Storage access","The bucket has Object Versioning turned on","The VM is in a different zone from the bucket","The service account needs the project Owner role before it can write objects"],0,"Legacy access scopes on a VM limit which APIs its credentials can use, even when IAM would allow more; setting the scope to cloud-platform and relying on IAM fixes this. Granting Owner is tempting, but it does not lift a scope restriction.","Domain 4: Service accounts and access scopes",[null,"Object Versioning keeps older versions and does not block uploads.","Buckets can be used from any zone; location does not cause scope errors.","The IAM role already allows uploads; the error names scopes, not IAM permissions."],3],["x54",0,4,"You enabled IAP TCP forwarding so administrators can SSH to VMs without external IPs, but connections time out. Which firewall change is needed?",["Allow ingress on TCP 22 from 0.0.0.0/0","Allow ingress on TCP 22 from IAP's range 35.235.240.0/20","Allow egress on TCP 443 to 0.0.0.0/0","Allow ingress on UDP 53 from the administrators' home IPs"],1,"IAP TCP forwarding connects to VMs from the 35.235.240.0/20 range, so an ingress rule allowing that range on port 22 is required. Opening 22 to everyone is tempting because it would work, but it exposes SSH to the internet.","Domain 4: Identity-Aware Proxy",["Opening SSH to the whole internet defeats the purpose of using IAP.",null,"Egress on 443 is not what IAP needs; IAP connects inbound to the VM's SSH port.","DNS on UDP 53 is unrelated to IAP's TCP forwarding to port 22."],3],["x55",0,4,"A public web app behind a global external Application Load Balancer is being hit by SQL injection attempts and traffic floods from a few countries. What should you add?",["Private Google Access on the backend subnet","A Cloud NAT gateway for the backends","A Cloud Armor security policy on the backend service","VPC Network Peering with a security vendor's VPC for inspection"],2,"Cloud Armor attaches to the load balancer's backend service and provides WAF rules, geo-based filtering and DDoS protection. Cloud NAT is tempting as a network security feature, but it only affects outbound traffic.","Domain 4: Network security",["Private Google Access lets internal VMs reach Google APIs and does not filter incoming web traffic.","Cloud NAT handles outbound connections from private VMs, not inbound attacks.",null,"Peering connects networks privately and does not inspect or block HTTP requests."],2],["x56",0,4,"Even users with valid IAM access must not be able to copy data from the company's BigQuery datasets and Cloud Storage buckets into projects outside the organization. Which control addresses this?",["Uniform bucket-level access on every bucket","Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) on every dataset and bucket","A billing budget per project","A VPC Service Controls perimeter around the projects"],3,"VPC Service Controls creates a service perimeter that blocks API access, such as copies, across the perimeter boundary, reducing data exfiltration risk. CMEK is tempting as a data protection control, but it does not stop authorized reads from being copied out.","Domain 4: Data exfiltration protection",["Uniform bucket-level access simplifies permissions but does not stop authorized users from copying data out.","CMEK controls the encryption keys, but authorized users can still read and copy decrypted data.","Budgets track spending and do not restrict data movement.",null],3],["x57",0,4,"You want a central view of misconfigurations across the organization, such as public buckets, open firewall ports and VMs with external IPs. What should you use?",["Security Command Center","Cloud Trace","Cloud Scheduler","Artifact Registry"],0,"Security Command Center reports misconfigurations and vulnerabilities across the organization, for example through Security Health Analytics. Artifact Registry is tempting because it can scan images, but it does not review bucket or firewall settings.","Domain 4: Security posture",[null,"Cloud Trace measures request latency and does not assess security settings.","Cloud Scheduler runs jobs on a schedule and does not find misconfigurations.","Artifact Registry stores container images and packages; it does not scan organization-wide settings."],2],["x58",0,4,"Project owners keep granting a sensitive permission to users. Security wants that permission blocked for everyone except a break-glass group, regardless of any allow policies. What should you use?",["A billing alert on the project","An IAM deny policy that exempts the break-glass group","A network tag on the project's VMs","A narrower custom role that leaves out that permission"],1,"IAM deny policies are evaluated before allow policies, so a denied permission cannot be used even if some role grants it, and exceptions can exempt specific principals. Offering a narrower role is tempting, but owners can still grant the broader one.","Domain 4: IAM deny policies",["Billing alerts report spending and cannot block permissions.",null,"Network tags apply to VM firewall rules and routes, not IAM permissions.","Creating another role does not stop owners from granting existing roles that include the permission."],3],["x59",0,4,"A symmetric Cloud KMS key protects application data, and policy requires a new key version every 90 days with no manual work. What should you configure?",["A Cloud Scheduler job that deletes the key every 90 days","A new key ring created by hand every quarter","An automatic rotation period of 90 days on the key","A service account key rotation script"],2,"Cloud KMS can rotate symmetric keys automatically on a set schedule, creating new primary versions while older versions stay available to decrypt existing data. Deleting and recreating keys is tempting as a reset, but it breaks access to encrypted data.","Domain 4: Cloud KMS",["Deleting the key would make data encrypted with it unreadable.","Creating key rings by hand is manual work and does not rotate the existing key.",null,"Service account keys are credentials for identities, not the encryption key that protects the data."],2],["x60",0,4,"An administrator tries to grant a role to partner@gmail.com on a project and gets an error that the member is not allowed. Other external domains are also rejected. What is the MOST likely cause?",["The partner's account has no billing account attached","The project has not enabled the IAM API","The partner must first be added as a Viewer at the organization","The domain restricted sharing org policy is enforced"],3,"Domain restricted sharing limits IAM grants to identities from allowed Cloud Identity or Workspace customers, rejecting outside accounts such as Gmail. Adding the user higher up the hierarchy is tempting, but the policy applies there too.","Domain 4: Organization policies for IAM",["Users do not need billing accounts to receive IAM roles on a project.","Granting IAM roles does not depend on enabling an API in the project.","Adding the account at the organization level would be blocked by the same policy.",null],3]]);
